window.harnessChunks["rows-42303484e96f046b.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-18T01:28:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dsifry/metaswarm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">metaswarm</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-18\">2026-11-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dsifry/metaswarm/tree/4bbb10abd301b9ec9d63371e296049cbc6043baa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4bbb10abd301b9ec9d63371e296049cbc6043baa\">4bbb10a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute model-mediated research, coding, tests, review responses and PR delivery for project work units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Evolving source code and tests, issue requirements, review feedback, setup errors and implementation uncertainties. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A coder/model agent chooses implementation changes and tool calls to meet the specific work unit definition and revises them in response to validator/reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: Installed host model-backed coder/issue agent executing the published metaswarm role/skill instructions, not the installer or static shell scripts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Work-unit plan, BEADS records, Git worktrees, subprocess tooling, run state, tests and CLI integration. - Closure path: Agent changes code, receives independent validation/review, iterates or escalates, and commits an accepted implementation/PR. - Boundary reachability: The installed start and orchestrated-execution skill explicitly binds an issue workflow to coding Task() roles and host tool calls; agent role payloads are part of the distributed plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Operational code-writing decisions are made by model-backed workers following source-shipped role requirements; ordinary Git and host API execution support their choices. - Evidence: skills/start/SKILL.md, skills/orchestrated-execution/SKILL.md, agents/coder.md, cli/metaswarm.js, skills/start/references/codex-tools.md - Basis: structural first-party shipped workflow and role contracts -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate identifiable write-surface and dependency interference between distinct parallel agent work units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independent agents can modify overlapping files/migrations, spawn conflicting branches, depend on unmerged work or overload shared worktree capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Swarm/issue coordinator judges file-scope and dependency conflicts, chooses to move, rebalance, serialize, pause or re-plan work, and transmits the resulting instructions/priority to affected agents. - Decision owner: Model-backed swarm coordinator in the shipped role and issue-orchestrator process; deterministic Git/BEADS tools persist the selected changes and validation gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-worktree assignments/conflict registry, Task/Team messaging and work-unit dependencies, plan review gate, branch collision tests and integration validation. - Closure path: Coordinator detects competing file/schema scope or unmet dependency, instructs subagents/revises the plan and allows dependent work only when prerequisites are complete; later work units obey revised ownership/sequence. - Boundary reachability: The plugin exposes swarm-coordinator agent role and shipped start/orchestrated execution skills in standard host task mode; prompts specify exact task assignment, conflict detection and returned operational commands. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited witness is role-directed conflict judgment and redistribution, not mere parallel spawning or message passing. - Evidence: agents/swarm-coordinator-agent.md, agents/issue-orchestrator.md, skills/orchestrated-execution/SKILL.md, skills/orchestrated-execution/guides/agent-coordination.md, skills/plan-review-gate/SKILL.md - Basis: explicit first-party role decision authority and conflict-specific workflow path - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Depends on configured host Task/Team tools and project-level worktree tracking; the docs are an operational contract rather than an independently observed concurrent run. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more coding subagents working on separate PR slices/issues in different worktrees under the same project orchestrator. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping file edits, schema changes or missing cross-branch dependency create merge/integration conflicts or block a sibling agent. - Attenuating coordination relation: First-party coordinator detects file/dependency overlaps; it may serialize, reassign worktree ownership, pause lower-priority work or broadcast conflicts; the plan gate rejects unsafe concurrent scopes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Remediation is sent to affected issue orchestrators/coders and changes subsequent assignment, worktree or wave execution before integration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Specific structurally modeled cross-agent source/migration collisions and corrective work redistribution exist, not only message envelopes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Manage current multi-issue fleet commitments, worktree capacity, priorities, active failures and escalation across the project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worktree saturation, active agent failure, urgent P0/P1 issues displacing lower-priority work, unresolvable conflicts and delayed delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Swarm coordinator can assign issues/worktrees, rebalance work, pause lower-priority work, spawn issue orchestrators and escalate problems beyond its authority. - Decision owner: Model-backed deployed swarm coordinator role under metaswarm instruction/host tooling, in its supported multi-issue orchestration mode; not a mere deterministic status counter. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Active assignments registry, worktree status, conflict registry, BEADS task status, agent heartbeats and host task orchestration. - Closure path: Coordinator reads present fleet state, chooses intervention and sends commands/revised task assignments to issue orchestrators; subsequent active agent work changes or waits for parent. - Boundary reachability: The shipped plugin role is installed and exposed through host-model invocation; the role&#39;s Decision Authority and Task/Team coordination mode supply the current-control return path for the project cell. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Coordinator is explicitly granted discretionary resource/priority decisions; monitoring registries only report current facts and do not own these choices. - Evidence: agents/swarm-coordinator-agent.md, skills/start/agents/swarm-coordinator-agent.md, skills/start/SKILL.md, skills/orchestrated-execution/guides/agent-coordination.md - Basis: explicit first-party role authority and operational workflow - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Conditional role activation in configured multi-issue mode; no live whole-project run observed. Human checkpoints are specific task gates and are not independently credited as a whole-system S3 parent mode. - Whole-system current view: Active GitHub Issues/BEADS epics, worktree utilization/assignments, conflict registry, heartbeats and blocked/failing agent state are specified coordinator inputs. - Current-control decision scope: Assignment, shared capacity, prioritization, pausing and rebalancing live work, with return via Task/Team messages and tracked worktree files.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently falsify implementer assertions using actual diffs, tests and independent assessment of each work-unit specification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementer hallucination, self-certification, incomplete or defective code, and review anchoring across retries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Fresh independent adversarial-review agent judges PASS/FAIL against work-unit spec and code evidence; orchestrator performs separate validation and adjudicates bounded retry/escalation. - Decision owner: New model-backed adversarial-reviewer per work unit/round; not implementer and not deterministic test runner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Spec/DoD, direct Git diff and tests, review rubric, attempt history, forced fresh-instance rule, gated commit. - Closure path: Reviewer FAIL produces actionable findings, implementation is revised and re-validated, and a new reviewer tests again; PASS permits commit while unresolved blockers force human escalation/stop. - Boundary reachability: orchestrated-execution and start shipped skill contracts mandate fresh host Task reviewer roles at concrete phase boundaries and route findings into the next implementation/review round. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer independently judges the implementation&#39;s actual code/DoD, rather than only displaying the implementing agent&#39;s own success report. - Evidence: skills/orchestrated-execution/SKILL.md, agents/adversarial-reviewer.md, skills/orchestrated-execution/rubrics/adversarial-review-rubric.md, skills/start/SKILL.md, skills/plan-review-gate/SKILL.md - Basis: first-party review role and explicit feedback/gate requirements - Confidence: medium-high for structurally required path - Caveats: No live audit of review independence beyond the source-defined fresh-Task separation; this is source-side S3 classification, not an independent OpenSiro second human audit. - Claim being audited: A coding subagent delivered a work unit fully and correctly under written spec/DoD. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder status, implementation output and self-described tests. - Complementary access path: Fresh separate reviewer reads actual diff/work-unit files and specification; orchestrator independently executes validation rather than believing coder self-report. - Independence boundary: Fresh Task instance on each review, not resumed teammate or implementing subagent, with prior findings deliberately withheld to avoid anchoring. - Who acts on findings: Implementing agent fixes FAILs, orchestrator revalidates and respawns reviewer; persistent blockers are escalated to the human.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Translate changing project/external tool feedback into accepted, persistent development knowledge that modifies the next agent&#39;s strategy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeated errors in GitHub review, CodeRabbit external feedback, third-party API quirks, changed build/test conventions and misunderstood user needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-assisted self-reflect proposes reusable lessons and ranks them; the project user explicitly selects which to accept or resolve against previous knowledge, retaining final prospective adaptation ownership. - Decision owner: Legitimate project operator for adopted knowledge choices; agent is the evidence-gathering/option-generating support, not an independent ultimate authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /self-reflect structured review/comment extraction, JSONL .beads knowledge writes, dedup/supersession, /prime selective lookup and subsequent planner/coder prompt consumption. - Closure path: External PR review/API experience → proposed lesson → human selection → committed project-local knowledge fact → /prime loads relevant rule into a later planning/coding session, changing how work is done. - Boundary reachability: The installable plugin ships both self-reflect and prime commands; orchestrated-execution requires knowledge capture before PR, and recovery/next-task instructions load relevant facts from the project knowledge base. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decisive acceptance/supersession of future policy-for-work belongs to the project human; automatic learning without approval is not asserted. - Evidence: commands/self-reflect.md, commands/prime.md, skills/orchestrated-execution/SKILL.md, skills/start/guides/delivery-scope.md, README.md - Basis: explicit end-to-end source-specified project mode and parent decision - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Source-level closed human decision and future priming route are shown, not observed behavior improvement on a live independent task. The curated knowledge affects project work practices, not proof of a self-governing S5 constitution. - External distinction: GitHub/CodeRabbit review feedback, external API behavior and user corrections are inputs distinct from the agent&#39;s ordinary transcript self-report. - Future / prospective distinction: Retained lessons explicitly target the next project task and future error prevention, not only a single-run memory summarization. - Adaptation option generated: Add/supersede a project-wide reusable rule or workaround, reject low-value patterns, or propose a separately approved metaswarm core issue. - Path back into current capability / S3: Once user-approved fact is written to .beads/knowledge, /prime reads it before subsequent planning/implementation and recovery, conditioning later agent behavior.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No accepted first-party organizational identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return loop is established at the installed project-cell recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Constitutional change of ultimate purpose, legitimate organizational rule-set and final identity authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped agent or parent constitutional policy procedure identified beyond project user checkpoints and fixed playbook guards. - Decision owner: None established for S5; project human decides checkpoints and S4 knowledge adoption but this does not create ultimate policy governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer/plan gates, human approvals, severity retry bounds, plugin role instructions and project knowledge rules. - Closure path: No distinct ultimate-policy change with authoritative return to the deployed project organization&#39;s constitution was traced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Mandatory local workflow guidelines and user-approved lessons are operational/adaptive constraints; they do not constitute a decision mechanism for the project organization&#39;s identity. - Evidence: skills/start/SKILL.md, skills/orchestrated-execution/SKILL.md, commands/self-reflect.md, cli/metaswarm.js, agents/swarm-coordinator-agent.md - Basis: bounded inspected packaged workflow surfaces -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0a9c21109add4c0ea00208177d182e40476b2e39/assessments/me/metaswarm.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0a9c21109add4c0ea00208177d182e40476b2e39\">0a9c211 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T20:13:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oh-my-pi</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/tree/dbf3afad4894bde827d90f965e77b3fe1c5a95e5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dbf3afad4894bde827d90f965e77b3fe1c5a95e5\">dbf3afa &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the primary and delegated coding agents autonomously perform useful repository/tool work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the first-party task surface exposes a model-selected isolated-worktree mode specifically addressing sibling write/merge interference; the parent agent chooses which spawned work is isolated while the runtime enforces separation and merge/patch handling. Peer Hub messaging additionally returns coordination feedback between distinct S1s, but generic messaging alone is not the basis for the positive state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the autonomous primary agent receives a current control surface through hub: it can observe its active peers/background commitments, cancel owned jobs, steer/revive peers through messaging, and start/stop/restart shared supervised processes. These interventions alter current capacity/commitments while deterministic registries/job managers/brokers enforce the decisions. Separately, the human Agent Hub exposes the current subagent roster and lets the parent steer, revive and kill members, establishing a distinct parent-governed S3 mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Advisor is a distinct reviewer actor with its own model/context/ToolSession and default independent workspace-inspection tools. It can challenge the primary trajectory and return findings that change subsequent execution through steering/blocker feedback. This supplies materially complementary access rather than merely re-reading the primary&#39;s final report.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: Advisor review, memory, model fallback and lifecycle maintenance concern present operation/quality; no separate prospective environment-facing adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: approval modes, user settings and human session control define operational permissions and configuration. No first-party identity/ultimate-policy matter with legitimate S5 closure was established, so generic user authority is not published as S5=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c/assessments/oh-my-pi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c\">3c76cda &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:17:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/the-open-engine/zeroshot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">zeroshot</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/the-open-engine/zeroshot/tree/4c2ed7d5cf3dce968ef7634a2bb49f4382226637\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4c2ed7d5cf3dce968ef7634a2bb49f4382226637\">4c2ed7d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement user-requested code changes or run research experiments with model-directed tool/file actions and return artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: arbitrary codebase tasks, repository state, model/tool feedback, tests and experiment outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: instantiated worker/researcher agent selects substantive coding or experiment actions; zeroshot graph invokes and retains the role under a specific runtime binding. - Decision owner: the first-party configured graph node&#39;s autonomous agent role through a supported harness adapter; external harness internals retain lower-level execution but are not separately scored. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/harness binding, graph transitions, workspace/capsule, runner, provider session, SQLite ledger and bounded retries. - Closure path: task input → worker/researcher model/tool action → workspace artifact/experiment evidence → result returned to graph and, after review, revised worker action or accepted output. - Boundary reachability: zeroshot run --template software-change and auto-research are built-in supported native CLI/Docker graph modes with first-party runner and first-party role prompts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: graph plumbing cannot implement semantic code changes or choose experiments without a model-driven worker; the autonomous operational actor is necessary. - Evidence: README; nativev2templates.rs; autoresearch.rs. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between independently active run/postmortem cells that share a scoped lesson store and might overwrite contradictory or stale guidance. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing runs and their postmortem maintenance/discovery agents, each producing prospective repository/graph advice for the same shared memory scope. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent updates to a lesson from stale snapshots, resurrecting a retired lesson, incompatible parallel advice replacing a newer record and duplicated overlapping recommendations. - Attenuating coordination relation: each specialist returns keep/adapt/retire/defer for frozen lesson IDs; host requires expected revisions and atomic publication, rejects conflicting writes, reloads the latest lesson snapshot, then re-invokes model reconciliation rather than accepting last-writer-wins. Same-scope overlap is consolidated; unrelated scopes cannot rewrite each other. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a publication conflict suspends the stale mutation and returns refreshed data to maintenance agents; only successfully reconciled newer cards become injectable guidance into subsequent model/tool sessions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the evidence identifies actual competing independent agent/run modifications of one shared knowledge resource and a conflict-specific reject/reconcile/return path, not merely a datastore or graph edge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cross-run retained-guidance collisions and stale-write interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide how to reconcile a stale/overlapping scoped lesson so concurrent-run advice can coexist or supersede safely. - Decision owner: first-party optimistic-concurrency and scoped specialist reconciliation constructor; autonomous maintenance judgments are available but the general inter-run arbitration mode remains scoped/composed, not a universal S2 organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned SQLite transactions, tombstones, stable IDs, scoped snapshots, conflict detection, specialist result schema and refresh/retry budget. - Closure path: concurrent postmortem decisions → CAS/version conflict → refreshed scope + specialist reconsideration → atomic accepted lesson revisions → future run retrieval or explicit failed reconciliation. - Boundary reachability: this is the supported local/Docker Active Memory path described in guide, implemented in postmortem.rs and store.rs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party mechanism explicitly constructs a recoverable S2-specific path; it does not establish broadly agent-owned coordination of every simultaneous run, so the conservative publication is C. - Evidence: Active Memory; postmortem.rs; store.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: conflict witness is shared retained lessons across runs, not all inter-agent workflow interference. Worker/reviewer fanout and graph scheduling alone are not used as S2 proof.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: manage the current auto-research program and its experiment/review/correction choices across distinct scouting and research S1 units; not all unrelated zeroshot installations. - Whole-system current view: one auto-research run&#39;s three scout proposals, current experiment/report, reviewer verdict and feedback, retained candidate and bounded iteration state. - Current-control decision scope: select which current approach/experiment to execute, revise work from review evidence, retain accepted result or stop a blocked/inconclusive round. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing scout directions, experimental failure, reviewer rejection and incomplete current research commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven researcher chooses experiment and revision priorities from scouts and review, while native graph enforces maximum iterations and terminal decisions. - Decision owner: first-party instantiated researcher Agent in the built-in auto-research graph; graph scheduler supplies deterministic execution constraints, and fresh reviewer provides challenge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Par scout fan-in, researcher continuation session, bounded inner review loop, current state/feedback fields, SQLite run ledger and optional push gate. - Closure path: three model scout proposals → researcher decision/experiment → fresh reviewer evidence → same researcher revises or accepts → next round&#39;s current plan/retained result. - Boundary reachability: auto-research native template with shipped researcher instructions is built-in, not a user-authored hypothetical graph. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model researcher owns current experiment selection and corrective judgment; removing it leaves fixed fan-in/limits but not the substantive cross-scout control choice. - Evidence: README auto-research; autoresearch.rs; researcher.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: S3=A is specific to the built-in multi-agent research-program mode. In software-change, fixed graph control and reviewer gates alone are not credited as an independent model-driven S3 controller.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: test the implementer&#39;s outcome through fresh independent acceptance/code reviewers, then force rework on rejection before delivery or successful closure. - Claim being audited: worker&#39;s code meets requested acceptance criteria and code-quality/correctness expectations, not merely that the worker completed a turn. - Ordinary reporting path: worker writes/edit files, returns output and claims completed implementation in its retained session. - Complementary access path: separate acceptance and code reviewer nodes run in parallel against the shared workspace, each with its own fresh model session and rubric; their structured verdict/diagnostic inputs are not copied from the worker&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: both reviewers start fresh on every pass while implementation/repair uses the same worker session; the native graph gives reviewer roles separate identities and returns both findings. - Who acts on findings: enforced graph routes rejected reviewer evidence back to original worker; the next pass repeats both independent reviewers. Delivery/CI conflicts may also return through controlled repair and re-review. Acceptance cannot bypass guards with a prose-only assertion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect self-reported success, acceptance gaps, code defects, regression and unreviewed delivery changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separately model-driven reviewer decides accepted/rejected criteria and can prevent progression, triggering another worker repair round. - Decision owner: first-party instantiated independent reviewer/verifier agents for the standard software-change mode; trusted native graph gates/runner enforce their verdicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed graph signals, Par reviewer join, guard routing, bounded global loop budget, retained worker session, fresh reviewer sessions, run ledger and CI/delivery gates. - Closure path: worker code → fresh acceptance+code review → accepted or rejection diagnostics → worker repair → new review pass → accepted artifact/delivery or bounded honest failure. - Boundary reachability: the default software-change template and review/repair continuity tests demonstrate a standard operational path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewers independently choose substantive feedback; graph machinery supplies independent session enforcement and corrective return, not semantic acceptance decisions itself. - Evidence: README; nativev2templates.rs; continuity.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: independence is of decision sessions/evidence roles, not guaranteed model-provider diversity or exhaustive correctness. User-specified graph/model settings can alter actual review coverage.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: discover and reconcile costly recurring errors and outdated procedural knowledge so later graph/repository agent work is better adapted to changed evidence. - External distinction: real repository files and tool receipts, repeated costly failures/rejected review loops, current scope content and changed advice provenance, rather than the just-finished worker&#39;s optimistic summary. - Future / prospective distinction: determine which recurring or newly learned guidance will remain useful across subsequent runs, adapt stale lessons to current source and retire obsoleted/conflicting advice. - Adaptation option generated: graph and repository discovery specialists propose new procedural cards; separate maintenance specialists keep/adapt/retire/defer all supplied lessons, consolidate overlaps and identify stable recommendations. - Path back into current capability / S3: versioned accepted cards are stored per graph/node/repository and injected through native pre-tool hooks or graph instructions in future sessions, changing model choices in later S1/S3 work without rewriting the authored graph or policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repetition of expensive mistakes, stale memory, changed code/graph context and loss of useful experience between runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: maintenance/discovery model specialists judge evidence-backed guidance changes; host validates cost/provenance, revisions and scope before publication. - Decision owner: standard-distribution postmortem model specialists in separate sessions, automatically enabled by default when Active Memory scopes and postmortem remain enabled; fixed validation/SQLite machinery enforces their decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool receipt capture, post-run outcome latch, per-scope indexes, strict cost threshold, version/CAS, bounded context, admission validation, tombstones, retrieval budgets and native hooks. - Closure path: run evidence/tool receipts → postmortem specialist review/discovery → validated revised scoped lessons → durable store → future run retrieval/injection → later model work and next postmortem reassessment. - Boundary reachability: the built-in Active Memory guide explicitly documents default enabled local/Docker paths; postmortem.rs, native supervisor, retrieval and hooks are source-backed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: specialist agents exercise context-sensitive keep/adapt/retire/discover discretion from actual evidence; host-only ranking/CAS cannot independently decide what lesson remains useful for future work. - Evidence: Active Memory; postmortem.rs; reconciliation tests; hooks.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: postmortem may abstain when evidence is inadequate, can fail independently of completed main graph, and requires working model/harness hooks. Evidence of a closed adaptive mechanism is not proof that a given lesson causally improves future task quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimate ultimate-identity/policy conflict adjudicator and authoritative return loop exists within a graph-run organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runtime scopes, model choices, graph contracts, delivery authorization and user approvals are fixed constraints rather than identity-level questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent or parent actor is first-party delegated ultimate purpose/identity choice at this recursion. - Decision owner: user/operator defines graph, tasks, credentials, publishing authority and scope; native runtime enforces these settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph validation, authored runtime plan, capsules, limits, run ledger, Git/CI policy and memory namespace controls. - Closure path: an operator can change configuration for later runs, but no outstanding identity-policy matter is routed to a legitimate S5 authority and returned through an explicit governance closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: graph profile names, model persona, user delivery ratification, or managed platform policy do not by themselves constitute identity or ultimate-policy decisions. - Evidence: README; graph reference; Active Memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0f8636f6b26e2e8f854dc02dd6102ef0ac8a32ec/assessments/ze/zeroshot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0f8636f6b26e2e8f854dc02dd6102ef0ac8a32ec\">0f8636f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:33:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/desplega-ai/agent-swarm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Swarm</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/desplega-ai/agent-swarm/tree/65e0ab6bb1f50f796b9c226e2bb34b5041cbaa66\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 65e0ab6bb1f50f796b9c226e2bb34b5041cbaa66\">65e0ab6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Operational delivery by distinct coding/research/review workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended implementation/research requests, uncertain tool results and task failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose task-local investigation, tool actions, implementation and completion/progress response. - Decision owner: The model-driven operational worker in the configured provider session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runner prompt assembly, provider adapters, MCP tools, task persistence and runtime limits transport/enforce worker decisions. - Closure path: Task → assembled context → worker tool/action choices → progress/result → task and lead follow-up. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model worker leaves scheduling/storage, not the same discretionary investigation or implementation. - Evidence: docker-compose.example.yml; src/commands/runner.ts (adapter.createSession and task handling); templates/official/coder/CLAUDE.md; src/prompts/base-prompt.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Provider access is required; the assessment does not import unexamined provider governance or claim successful business output.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference among concurrent S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New requests arrive through different ingresses while related work remains in flight, risking duplicate or contradictory implementation/review. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Interpret sibling intent, coordinate with named workers, avoid duplicate action, and choose compatible task work. - Decision owner: Lead and operational model workers own the coordination judgment; the sibling detector does not decide task semantics. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: contextKey lookup, in-flight task status, original-intent rendering, messaging/task tools, same-agent parent-session selection and lead no-duplicate rules. - Closure path: Same-context sibling discovery → task prompt lists owners/status/original requests and instructs coordinate/do not duplicate → agent queries/messages/reuses ongoing work → subsequent assignment/action changes. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sibling context intentionally exposes this inter-worker disturbance; the agent decides what overlaps and what should change. - Evidence: src/tasks/sibling-awareness.ts; src/tasks/sibling-block.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md rules 2, 7, 8; src/tools/send-task.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Structural disturbance witness, not a claim that every collision is prevented. Incorrect/missing context keys can miss related work. - Distinct S1 units: Separately assigned coder, researcher and reviewer workers in the official multi-worker deployment. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent same-context tasks from multiple ingresses can duplicate or conflict with each other&#39;s unfinished work; same-PR reviews can be double-assigned. - Attenuating coordination relation: Named sibling tasks and status are supplied to the new worker; explicit coordinate/do-not-duplicate instructions plus task inspection/messaging let it adjust alongside lead deduplication. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Coordination changes which work a new task performs and whether lead creates/routes another task; same-agent continuation can resume existing context. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party renderer explicitly detects concurrent related work, describes its original intent and instructs interference attenuation. Generic mailboxes or dependsOn alone are not the witness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Control the team&#39;s inside-and-now commitments and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled sessions, failed workers, unclaimed recovery pins, affinity-starved tasks and abandoned commitments across the team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Assess current state and choose reroute, retry, cancel, recreate or intervention rather than blindly continue. - Decision owner: The official lead agent owns discretionary current-control decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Heartbeat preflight and code triage detect/escalate; snapshots, task creation, steer/cancel/send tools enforce the selected decision. - Closure path: Whole-team status and standing orders → lead heartbeat/reroute-decision task or worker-failure follow-up → lead investigates and chooses corrective task/resource action → task service changes subsequent worker operation. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Automatic retry thresholds and transactions expose exceptional state; lead must choose the organizational response after those mechanisms cannot settle it. - Evidence: src/heartbeat/heartbeat.ts; src/heartbeat/templates.ts; templates/official/lead/HEARTBEAT.md; src/tasks/worker-follow-up.ts; src/tools/templates.ts task.worker.failed; src/tools/steer-task.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: A parent can administer deployment, but isolated configuration or approval is not counted as a distinct evidenced parent S3 mode. - Whole-system current view: Gathered agents, active/pending/failed tasks and stalled sessions, combined with lead standing orders and full task-detail access. - Current-control decision scope: Team commitments and recovery: cancel obsolete work, reassign eligible workers, steer current execution, recreate still-needed failed work and avoid retry storms.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complementary audit of an operational code-delivery claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coder can report completion while code contains security/race/state-machine faults or inadequate evidence/tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Form an independent review judgment with blocking findings/request-changes and return corrective requirements. - Decision owner: A distinct official reviewer model session owns audit judgment; coder/lead own correction and subsequent work routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate worker environment/task, direct git checkout, GitHub review/CI access, task completion/follow-up and parentTaskId continuity. - Closure path: Lead routes a review to reviewer → reviewer independently clones/checks out actual PR code and examines tests/CI → blocking findings/request changes → coder resolves feedback with parentTaskId continuity; lead can route corrective work. Human approval remains a separate merge requirement. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer directly inspects operational evidence in a separate role/session and decides substantive findings, beyond the implementer&#39;s summary or deterministic CI verdict. - Evidence: docker-compose.example.yml; templates/official/reviewer/CLAUDE.md; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md routing/review rules; templates/official/coder/CLAUDE.md review-feedback rule; src/tasks/worker-follow-up.ts; src/tools/templates.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: This is a supported role-based audit path when lead assigns review, not proof every task is audited. The ordinary 500-character completion follow-up alone is not complementary audit; shared credentials/model family are not institutional independence. - Claim being audited: Coder&#39;s proposed change is ready/correct and tests cover changed paths. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder progress/completion and a summary follow-up task to lead. - Complementary access path: Reviewer must clone repository and check out the PR branch, inspect actual code, tests and CI; not only read the coder&#39;s summary/diff. - Independence boundary: Separate reviewer worker/session and instructions, distinct from the producing coder; official deployment includes the reviewer. - Who acts on findings: Reviewer requests changes with concrete blocking fixes; coder resolves review feedback in the originating task context, with lead routing and subsequent review as needed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Outside-and-then intelligence for the deployed team&#39;s own GTM campaign. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing discovery channels, search demand and content opportunities make the team&#39;s existing GTM plan less effective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Turn external trend distinctions into candidate future campaign/content adaptations; adopter must still compose selection and implementation closure. - Decision owner: The configured lead produces options; deployment owner must supply campaign goal, integrations and the decisive adoption/return-to-S3 relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party parameterized weekly schedule prompt, GitHub/GSC integrations, configured GTM plan/state, admin report and saved research workspace. - Closure path: Configured own-campaign metrics → week-over-week search/referrer/content analysis → what-to-try-next and gap/opportunity options → report. Turning that report into an adopted current-capability change is intentionally not supplied as an automatic closed mode. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A function-specific first-party prospective adaptation constructor exists, but no closed agent-owned or parent-owned adoption loop is established by the template. - Evidence: templates/schedules/gtm-weekly-review/config.json; templates/schedules/gtm-weekly-review/content.md; templates/official/discoverability-optimizer/SOUL.md. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Optional template; requires own GTM goal, REPOURL/GSCPROPERTY and integrations. Ordinary customer research as a delivered task, generic memory, persona edits and arbitrary extensions are not S4 witnesses. - External distinction: Repository traffic/referrers/popular paths and Search Console queries, impressions, CTR and rankings for the deployment&#39;s configured campaign. - Future / prospective distinction: Week-over-week change, high-impression low-CTR/rank opportunities, content gaps and what to try next against the current GTM plan. - Adaptation option generated: Candidate SEO/content/campaign experiments to improve the team&#39;s own future reach. - Path back into current capability / S3: Report through configured admin/in-app channels and saved research workspace intentionally exposes options to the deployment; actor authority and adoption/execution closure must be composed, so C rather than A/P.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity and ultimate-policy adjudication at deployed-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A prospective conflict about the team&#39;s constitutive purpose or ultimate policy would require authoritative settlement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive identity/ultimate-policy arbitration and return loop is supplied in the inspected deployed surfaces. - Decision owner: No in-boundary S5 owner established; operator configuration and lead profile editing are not sufficient evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL/IDENTITY/CLAUDE persistence, update-profile, repo guidelines, permission/budget and extension activation controls. - Closure path: Observed paths persist persona, operational checks and permissions; they do not reconstruct a disputed identity/ultimate-policy matter being authoritatively settled and governing future operation. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Neither writable identity files nor final human say on individual merges establishes the S5 function. - Evidence: docs-site/content/docs/(documentation)/architecture/agents.mdx/architecture/agents.mdx); src/tools/update-profile.ts; src/tools/repos/update-repo.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md; src/prompts/base-prompt.ts; docs-site/content/docs/(documentation)/guides/extensions.mdx/guides/extensions.mdx). - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Bounded absence in this deployed distribution, not an impossibility claim for a separately composed organization&#39;s governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/548479149bdce4a226ecd139c0a8c4f7f244b2c7/assessments/ag/agent-swarm.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 548479149bdce4a226ecd139c0a8c4f7f244b2c7\">5484791 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T03:07:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gastownhall/gastown\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gas Town</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gastownhall/gastown/tree/649b832b7672bc7a2dbef26f5983aba6198b819b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 649b832b7672bc7a2dbef26f5983aba6198b819b\">649b832 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement bounded software-engineering issues in project repositories and return completed branch/work results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, repository state, implementation choices, tool/test observations, local blockers and discovered defects within an assigned issue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive implementation/research/debugging actions required to satisfy the hooked issue and decide when the bounded work is ready to submit through the prescribed completion path. - Decision owner: the model-driven Polecat worker agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated git worktrees, hooked Beads assignments, role/formula context, tool-capable model runtime, direct issue state updates and gt done completion plumbing. - Closure path: Mayor/runtime hooks an issue to a Polecat → Polecat executes autonomously in its isolated worktree → commits/pushes and runs the completion protocol → branch/result enters the downstream Witness/Refinery or configured PR path. - Boundary reachability: Polecat role templates, worktree provisioning, hook state and completion commands are shipped first-party Gas Town surfaces at the pinned revision; the external model host supplies inference but does not replace Gas Town&#39;s first-party worker contract. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the role and formula constrain execution, but the model-driven worker chooses substantive code/tool actions and how to solve the assigned issue. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/polecat.md.tmpl, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate integration interference among independently produced Polecat branches so concurrent work does not collide on a stale common baseline or land in an incompatible order. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple autonomous workers can finish branches from earlier target states; parallel/direct merging can create conflicts, invalid dependency order or inconsistent integration as the target branch moves after each accepted change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next branch/merge order using priority, dependencies and timing; rebase it against the current target; resolve or reject conflicts; and require each subsequent branch to rebase against the newly advanced target. - Decision owner: the model-driven Refinery agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-rig merge queue, MERGEREADY protocol, isolated worker branches, sequential-rebase rule, target-resolution rules and merge-request state. - Closure path: multiple Polecat outputs enter the queue → Refinery selects one and rebases it on the current target → accepted merge advances the target → the next queued branch is rebased against that new baseline; unresolvable conflicts reopen the source issue and return through Witness for new work. - Boundary reachability: the canonical Refinery role and merge queue are shipped first-party operating surfaces for non-fork rigs; the role contract explicitly assigns merge/conflict/order judgment to the running agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic queue/state machinery exposes and enforces the integration surface, but the first-party Refinery contract gives the model agent discretion over ordering and conflict/failure disposition; this is not merely authored static sequencing. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/refinery.md.tmpl, docs/design/mail-protocol.md, internal/refinery/batch.go. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: isolated worktrees by themselves would only be supporting interference attenuation; the A classification rests on the agent-owned sequential integration feedback loop. - Distinct S1 units: two or more autonomous Polecats completing separate issues/branches in the same rig or integration target. - Inter-S1 disturbance: their branches can be based on the same earlier target and therefore conflict, invalidate each other&#39;s assumptions or require dependency-sensitive ordering when integrated. - Attenuating coordination relation: Refinery serially selects, rebases and integrates branches against the current moving target instead of allowing parallel direct merges. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: failed/conflicting work reopens its source issue and returns through Witness for re-sling/rework; successful integration changes the baseline against which later queued work is evaluated. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the cited relation addresses a concrete interference mode among outputs of independent S1 workers — incompatible concurrent branch integration — and feeds conflict/failure results back into later operational work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the town&#39;s current work commitments, dispatch, cross-rig allocation, escalations and active execution on behalf of the whole Gas Town workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unassigned ready work, stalled or failed workers, competing/cross-rig work, blocked dependencies, changed priorities, integration/escalation problems and idle or misallocated execution capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what work to dispatch and to which rig/worker, coordinate batch/cross-rig work, resolve escalations that Witnesses cannot handle, redirect priorities/integration decisions and intervene when current agents or rigs are stuck. - Decision owner: the model-driven Mayor agent in the standard autonomous mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: town/rig status, convoys, Beads dependencies, mail/escalation state, Polecat lists, hooks, gt sling, scheduler capacity support and Witness/Deacon feedback. - Closure path: current town/rig/convoy/escalation state becomes visible to Mayor → Mayor makes dispatch/coordination/intervention decisions → Gas Town creates/hooks workers or routes instructions/escalations → Polecat/Witness/Refinery/Deacon behavior proceeds under the changed current state and reports back. - Boundary reachability: Gas Town ships a dedicated Mayor session, first-party Mayor role template and control commands; Mayor is launched as an agent and connected to the same persisted work/communication surfaces used by the operational organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scheduler and hook mechanisms constrain execution, but the global dispatch, cross-rig judgment and escalation-resolution rights are assigned to the model-driven Mayor rather than being fully predetermined by runtime code. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/cmd/mayor.go, README.md, docs/design/escalation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the human Overseer can interact with or administratively operate Gas Town, but the reviewed evidence does not establish a sufficiently explicit distinct parent S3 operating mode comparable to a first-party approval/override lifecycle; ordinary human tasking and process controls are therefore not encoded as A(P). - Whole-system current view: Mayor has first-party commands and persisted state for overall town status, rigs, Polecats, active convoys, issue/dependency readiness, mail and escalations across the workspace. - Current-control decision scope: current work dispatch, worker/rig routing, batch/cross-rig coordination, escalation resolution, priority/integration judgments and intervention on stuck execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a Polecat&#39;s completion claim before its branch is allowed to land and return corrective action when the integrated result is not acceptable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer self-verification can miss failures that appear only after rebasing onto the current target, integration conflicts, branch-caused test failures or untracked pre-existing failures that would otherwise be merged without an independent gate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: run the configured verification suite after rebase, decide whether failure is branch-caused or pre-existing, block/reject a failing branch, reopen its source issue and require returned rework, or allow a verified branch to merge. - Decision owner: the separate model-driven Refinery agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MERGEREADY handoff from Witness, separate Refinery session/role, queue state, rebase onto current target, configured setup/typecheck/lint/build/test commands, mandatory handle-failures gate and MERGEFAILED/MERGED return protocol. - Closure path: Polecat completion passes through Witness → Refinery obtains the branch independently and rebases/tests it → pass permits merge; branch-caused failure reopens source issue, closes the merge request and notifies Witness → Witness/current-control machinery can re-sling corrected operational work. - Boundary reachability: the verification gate is embedded in the shipped canonical Refinery patrol contract and directly controls whether standard non-fork work reaches the target branch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tests provide evidence but do not decide the organizational response; Refinery independently interprets conflict/failure provenance and owns the block/reopen/merge judgment under a role explicitly separated from application development. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/refinery.md.tmpl, docs/design/mail-protocol.md, internal/templates/roles/witness.md.tmpl. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the same Refinery actor also owns S2 integration ordering, but the S3 claim uses a distinct decision right: independent acceptance/challenge of a producer completion claim and corrective return, not merge sequencing itself. - Claim being audited: that a Polecat&#39;s submitted branch is integration-safe and satisfies the configured quality/test checks sufficiently to land on the target branch. - Ordinary reporting path: Polecat performs its own completion/self-verification and signals done; Witness processes completion/lifecycle state and sends MERGEREADY. - Complementary access path: Refinery independently checks the queued branch after rebasing onto the current target and runs the configured verification commands in its own merge-processing session. - Independence boundary: Refinery is a separate agent role and is explicitly forbidden from acting as the application developer; it does not simply accept the producer&#39;s self-report and can block landing/reopen work. - Who acts on findings: Refinery directly blocks or merges; on failure it reopens the source issue and notifies Witness, which can return the work to a new/current operational worker.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no materially separate first-party external/prospective adaptation function is established at the reviewed town boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external ecosystem change, future project/runtime conditions, model/provider evolution and strategic capability adaptation were inspected, but no qualifying shipped S4 owner and closed return loop was found. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established that continuously or episodically distinguishes outside/future conditions, develops adaptation options and returns a selected organizational adaptation into present Gas Town capability/S3. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mayor strategic/integration judgments, shared memories, project/research documents, HOP/Gas City material, scheduler state and historical capability/ledger data can inform work but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: no qualifying outside/future sensing → adaptation option → present capability/current-control return path is established in the credited standard operating surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Mayor&#39;s use of the word strategic concerns current architecture, priority and integration decisions in the reviewed role contract; without a distinct external/prospective sensing-and-adaptation loop it is not promoted to S4. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/cmd/mayor.go, README.md, reviewed design/research surfaces. - Basis: explicit + absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no materially separate first-party organizational identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the declared Gas Town workspace boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user intent, architecture/priorities, role templates, directives/configuration and Overseer interaction were inspected, but no qualifying town-level identity dispute/policy authority with a closed S5 return path was established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for choosing or ratifying the ultimate purpose/identity/constitutional policy of the Gas Town organization as distinct from assigning work, configuring roles or exercising current operational control. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the human Overseer, Mayor interface, role/formula templates, project configuration, directives/overlays, rig lifecycle controls and user instructions shape the system but are not sufficient S5 ownership by themselves. - Closure path: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision lifecycle comparable to goal/constitution/strategy ratification and return into autonomous operation was found at the pin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming the human as Overseer, routing strategic questions to Mayor, or allowing an operator to configure/stop/dock agents does not by itself establish S5; those paths are tasking, configuration or current control unless the ultimate organizational identity decision is explicit. - Evidence: internal/cmd/mayor.go, internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/templates/roles/deacon.md.tmpl, reviewed configuration/directive surfaces. - Basis: explicit + absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7e14b6da80b9c974758f325654da2d00a7785624/assessments/gastown.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7e14b6da80b9c974758f325654da2d00a7785624\">7e14b6d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-16T21:07:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/vstorm-co/pydantic-deepagents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pydantic Deep</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-16\">2026-11-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/vstorm-co/pydantic-deepagents/tree/650b592b6accd7dc994226abbaa0cf717527e457\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 650b592b6accd7dc994226abbaa0cf717527e457\">650b592 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Complete coding and workspace tasks through model-selected tool action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain requirements, changing workspace observations and tool results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose what to inspect, edit or execute and choose subsequent actions from tool feedback. - Decision owner: The configured model-backed Pydantic Deep agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pydantic AI execution, installed CLI, workspace, toolsets and usage limits. - Closure path: The CLI passes the task to the constructed agent run and returns the resulting answer. During the run, tool responses inform the next action. - Boundary reachability: The package exposes the CLI and its headless entry calls the shipped agent factory and run directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves configured capabilities and limits but removes discretionary task action. - Evidence: pyproject.toml; apps/cli/run.py; apps/cli/agent.py; pydanticdeep/agent.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: High for source reachability. - Caveats: Requires a configured compatible model and operating workspace.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent simultaneously executing alternate coding branches from contaminating each other&#39;s workspace state during exploration and reconvergence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing branches may independently edit the same destination path, with incompatible results or a write that can distort another running branch&#39;s fallback read. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect the active fork and choose which branch&#39;s changes may become shared state, or discard the fork. - Decision owner: The model-backed parent-run agent in the agent-driven mode. Deterministic workspace isolation maintains separation during exploration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-branch overlays, changed-path comparison, merge lock, and cancellation before shared writes. - Closure path: Branch-local writes feed subsequent branch-local reads. At convergence the chosen overlay is flushed, competitors are quiesced before they can observe that flush, and the surviving context governs continued work. - Boundary reachability: Interactive CLI enables forking by default and registers shipped fork, diff, inspect and resolve tools. SDK provides the same opt-in capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The branch views and quiescence rule are deterministic. Choice of the mutually exclusive active path is delegated to the model-backed parent agent in the claimed mode. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more separately tasked branches, each running a model loop in its own cloned dependencies and workspace. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Incompatible writes to the same relative path and visibility of an adopted version to another still-running branch. - Attenuating coordination relation: Local overlay containment, cross-branch diff and exactly one admitted overlay with other branches cancelled. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each branch reads its own state while running. At convergence the selected workspace becomes the continuation baseline and competing writers stop. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The implemented relation addresses an identified shared-file interference path and changes which state can enter an active work unit. - Evidence: pydanticdeep/features/forking/coordinator.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/isolation.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/diff.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/init.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/coordinator.py; apps/cli/commands.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: Moderate to high in live workspace-fork mode. - Caveats: Shared TODO state and team messaging are additional surfaces; they are not the S2 witness used here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the current fork&#39;s active commitments across its model-driven work branches. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Running, failed or cost-exhausted branches competing to continue a single active job. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Use the current fork-wide status and cost view to terminate branches or abort, leaving a changed portfolio of ongoing commitments. - Decision owner: Model-backed lead in the agent-tool mode, and session operator in the separate TUI mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Fork summary, branch status and cost tracking, stop/merge tools and preconfigured budget caps. - Closure path: Cancellation updates the branch lifecycle and removes its consumption. An abort releases the fork or a picked branch returns a selected history and workspace to the parent run. - Boundary reachability: The shipped agent and TUI expose the same fork runtime and explicit intervention controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: In agent mode the agent can decide whether and when to terminate or abandon existing work; automatic caps merely enforce their presets. The TUI supplies a separate operator-owned path. - Whole-system current view: At declared fork-session recursion, inspection spans every active branch and the fork cost view aggregates the branches. - Current-control decision scope: Intervene in running branch commitments, abandon the fork or authorize the surviving commitment. - Evidence: pydanticdeep/features/forking/init.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/init.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/init.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/coordinator.py; apps/cli/forking.py; apps/cli/commands.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: Moderate to high at session recursion. - Caveats: No cross-tenant or multi-project resource authority is inferred.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Corroborate branch claims against materialized changes and optional separate test execution before controlling fork resolution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A branch&#39;s textual report may overstate task completion or disagree with the edited artifacts or measured outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately instantiated judge assesses the actual branch evidence and selects the supported winning branch in auto/vote mode. - Decision owner: First-party model-backed judge, or the model panel in vote mode with deterministic aggregation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Structured outcomes, independent diff collection, optional coordinator-run branch tests, structured verdict and merge dispatcher. - Closure path: The judge verdict selects the branch to adopt in auto/vote mode, causing application of its overlay and cancellation of competitors. CLI replays the selected history. - Boundary reachability: The shipped CLI exposes the automated merge strategies and routes the coordinator&#39;s committed result into session state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The judge owns the evaluative selection. Test execution, diff computation and application of the verdict are supporting machinery. - Claim being audited: Whether a branch actually fulfilled its assigned task. - Ordinary reporting path: The branch&#39;s own final assistant message and run counters. - Complementary access path: The coordinator assembles changed-file diffs directly from workspace state and, where configured, runs a separate test command against the branch snapshot. - Independence boundary: A new judge request receives the branch goal, bounded reports and complementary evidence rather than participating in the coding turn or relying on the branch narrative alone. - Who acts on findings: The coordinator commits the selected outcome in auto/vote mode, and the session continues on the adopted state. - Evidence: pydanticdeep/features/forking/coordinator.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/coordinator.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/coordinator.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/judge.py; pydanticdeep/features/forking/judge.py; apps/cli/commands.py; apps/cli/commands.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: Moderate to high for the source path. - Caveats: Optional live fork audit. Local test execution requires a compatible workspace and detected or configured test command. Manual branch selection does not exercise this automated audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Adapt future coding behavior from actual encounters with the project&#39;s environment, build commands and toolchain. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Project-specific workflow and environment differences encountered across sessions which would otherwise cause repeated ineffective approaches. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select the proposed durable adaptation to admit into project guidance after reviewing the synthesis report. - Decision owner: The project session operator. Extraction and synthesis agents propose the options. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session archive, raw tool traces, extraction and synthesis models, review modal and context file loading. - Closure path: The operator selects the proposed guidance, the CLI writes selected changes to project context, and the supported local-workspace mode reloads context for subsequent agent work. - Boundary reachability: The installed TUI includes /improve, admission by selection and the first-party context reader. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The analyzer can choose what to propose. Its installed automatic-analysis path does not own the persistence decision in the evidenced loop. - External distinction: Workspaces, build/test commands, project tools and dependencies encountered outside the harness, captured in tool outcomes. - Future / prospective distinction: The prompt explicitly identifies reusable strategies for later sessions and recurring project situations. - Adaptation option generated: For example a proposed reusable build/test practice in AGENTS.md based on observed first-party session traces. - Path back into current capability / S3: Operator review selects applicable proposals, the analyzer changes the project instructions, and the agent incorporates the changed context during subsequent work. - Evidence: pydanticdeep/features/improve/prompts.py; pydanticdeep/features/improve/prompts.py; pydanticdeep/features/improve/analyzer.py; apps/cli/commands.py; pydanticdeep/features/improve/analyzer.py; pydanticdeep/features/context/capability.py; apps/cli/agent.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: Moderate for the local CLI project-guidance mode. - Caveats: Assessment does not infer closed adaptation from memory persistence alone or claim the configured SDK always enables improvement.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No supported ultimate-policy adjudication is evidenced at the assessed session/workspace level. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Project style, task permissions and fork conflicts pass through lower-level configuration, approvals and control decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped escalation from a conflict over the harness&#39;s ultimate purpose or risk policy into a binding adjudication was identified. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner is established inside the reviewed runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Configurable instructions, approvals, instruction discovery and apply-selected context changes. - Closure path: Their returned decisions govern the project task, future techniques or style. The shipped paths do not adjudicate the harness&#39;s ultimate identity/authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The available agent and parent paths are scoped to operations, branch control and prospective project guidance. - Evidence: pydanticdeep/features/improve/prompts.py; pydanticdeep/features/context/service.py; apps/cli/config.py; apps/cli/commands.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: Moderate within the examined distribution. - Caveats: Repository maintainers and host application owners are outside this session recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f1c0cc3697e767ad88adeb2b2bfbe844d2fed6b5/assessments/py/pydantic-deep.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f1c0cc3697e767ad88adeb2b2bfbe844d2fed6b5\">f1c0cc3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T19:14:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Muvon/octomind\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Octomind</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Muvon/octomind/tree/fceb8cbe970d2ad097e59d266ae915a3ebb43779\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fceb8cbe970d2ad097e59d266ae915a3ebb43779\">fceb8cb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user objectives through model-directed local/MCP tool actions in an adaptive session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source tree, changing command results, failed tests, tool errors, external API responses and task-specific observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses the next tool, argument and work step after receiving prior action output. - Decision owner: first-party specialist actor in Octomind&#39;s session/chat runtime; the selected external model supplies inference, not a separate vendor harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: native Rust chat session, MCP call dispatch, role/tool config, session history, guardrails, compression, supervision and provider client. - Closure path: task→session model request→model tool instruction→MCP/native executor→recorded tool result→subsequent model turn or terminal result. - Boundary reachability: ordinary installed CLI and piped/daemon session modes enter same first-party model/tool session path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action choices depend on model reasoning about live observations, rather than being only a prewritten deterministic script; policy and tool mediation constrain choices but do not invent them. - Evidence: src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/session/chat/response/toolexecution.rs, src/mcp/core/functions.rs, README.md. - Basis: source-backed installed autonomous specialist. - Confidence: high for runnable first-party code structure, not model task performance. - Caveats: tap-specific tools, hosted model weights and endpoint success are not part of ownership attribution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately demonstrated first-party inter-S1 interference-attenuation relation within the selected coding-session and optional specialist group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: nested/dynamic agents can contend for model capacity or shared files; concrete conflicts are plausible but not shown as a closed native feedback relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inter-agent conflict-specific arbitration that changes affected S1 behavior after detecting interference; not established by inspected paths. - Decision owner: supervisor handles the whole session&#39;s plan; child delegation and MCP orchestration communicate tasks/results without proven distinctive inter-S1 collision arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate, MCP agent invocation, monitored tool processes, role constraints, generic authorizer/cost bounds and session/workdir state. - Closure path: child task/return and supervisor&#39;s whole-current plan changes do not independently form an S2-specific conflicting-worker attenuation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a plan owner, tool batch authorization, per-role access bounds or generic delegation do not prove shared-resource contention detection and corrective return between separate operational divisions. - Evidence: src/supervisor/delegate.rs, src/mcp/agent/functions.rs, src/mcp/orchestration/monitor.rs, src/supervisor/plan.rs. - Basis: bounded negative source inspection. - Confidence: moderate at selected recursion level. - Caveats: additional externally configured multi-agent topology might bring S2 evidence, but that would require a concrete first-party collision-specific path and return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: separate model-based whole-current supervisor of active specialist plan/phase commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled work, unverified phase completion, broken assumptions or changed trajectory within a multi-phase objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: external supervisor decides when to create, advance, hold or revise the operative phase plan according to runtime action evidence and original user outcome constraints. - Decision owner: first-party out-of-band supervisor model planner; runtime validates and applies its structured decision. The specialist does not possess the plan mutation tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan signals, per-turn preparation, callsupervisorjson, PlanDecision, sidecarstart/advance/revise, injected authoritative plan notes and bounded error latches. - Closure path: runtime-observed action/phase evidence→external planner decision→persistent current plan transition or evidence-grounded hold→runtime-plan note to model-directed specialist→subsequent task decisions occur under revised current phase. - Boundary reachability: first-party supervisor plan is enabled in shipped default config for qualifying complex tasks; trivial/answer-only jobs intentionally bypass it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct model decides actual whole-task phase regulation and returned current commitments, not a fixed progress counter or human-only approval. A concerns this documented current-control mode only. - Evidence: src/supervisor/plan.rs, src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/mcp/core/functions.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - Whole-system current view: standing instructions, original task, active plan, phase trajectory, evidence ledger and outcome conditions, beyond a single pending model tool call. - Current-control decision scope: choose and revise current task phase commitments and prevent unjustified advancement despite specialist claims. - Basis: source-confirmed distinct autonomous supervisor decision/return. - Confidence: high for structural ownership and default enablement, without live demonstration. - Caveats: planner intentionally declines simple requests, failed model calls can leave bookkeeping unchanged, and no autonomy is imported from a third-party agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: out-of-band supervisor-model verification of producer specialist&#39;s claimed done/completion against independent runtime action evidence and artifacts, with returned corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, missing tests, unsupported work claims and regressions hidden by optimistic specialist narration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separately tasked verifier determines Pass/Gaps/Indeterminate, with challenged findings able to prevent verified completion and re-enter correction. - Decision owner: distinct Octomind supervisor verification model invocation (not the specialist&#39;s own status token); first-party runtime owns the binding verdict gate and reentry budget. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime action ledger, readback output, task/claim, ground-truth Git state, structured decision schema, independent refutation, bounded reentry and learning outcome labels. - Closure path: specialist self-reports done→first-party runtime captures actions/artifacts→separate supervisor verifier judges them→Gaps returned to specialist for bounded correction or explicit unverified outcome; Pass finalizes verifiable claim/plan and logs verified outcome. - Boundary reachability: enabled supervisor/gate in packaged default TOML, called at qualifying actual completion claims in the session executor; not off-box CI or benchmark scoring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct model performs substantive correctness judgment with access to runtime-owned evidence not controlled by producer, rather than mere transcript logging or same-agent self-review. - Evidence: src/supervisor/gate.rs, src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/supervisor/detect.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - Claim being audited: requested coding task is actually completed and outcome conditions hold. - Ordinary reporting path: specialist done token, user-facing summary and tool outputs. - Complementary access path: separate supervisor builds task/evidence packet from session action ledger, Git state and tool readbacks; can independently request missing output. - Independence boundary: independent role/model call and runtime-furnished ledger rather than specialist self-report, though same configured model-provider infrastructure may serve both. - Who acts on findings: runtime applies completion gate, new specialist correction prompt or unverified handoff; successful gate finalizes plan and supplies outcome credit. - Basis: source-confirmed agent decision and binding first-party corrective return. - Confidence: high for wired audit constructor, live model quality untested. - Caveats: abstention/timeout is not evidence of passing; no claim that an independent reviewer actually ran in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: when operator enables optional evolution, external and cross-session observations are synthesized into proposed changes of future-session skills/guardrails; first-party counterfactual evaluation determines trial/promotion/retirement of generated behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurrent user constraints, observed repeated failure patterns, newly learned project rules and changed usefulness/cost of optional capabilities across future sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate supervisor model may autonomously synthesize an advisory skill candidate from grounded lessons; a first-party counterfactual trial judges whether activating it improves future outcomes, promotes an effective candidate or retires weak/regressed behavior. Parent controls enablement, effectful trials and explicit approval/reject/rollback. - Decision owner: detached learning supervisor plus native promotion/pruning policy own the autonomous S4 path; operator supplies initial opt-in and effectful authorizations, so supplementary parent (P) is explicitly recorded. User approval is not confused with autonomous model judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source-quote and outcome-grounded Lesson, per-project store + scope, synthesizeafterextraction/synthesizestoreifdue, schema verification/replay checks, candidate registry, Shadow→Trial→Active/Retired, held-out counterfactual control/treatment, initforsession and generated skill/guardrail runtime merges. - Closure path: verified previous-session evidence→deduped reusable lessons→separate supervisor chooses a capability candidate→native schema/replay validation→shadow sample of future tasks without application→gated live treatment with counterfactual results→promoted skill/guard accessible to new sessions (or retired candidate)→actual future behavior changes and continues receiving evidence. - Boundary reachability: this is installed first-party [supervisor.learning.evolution] enabled = true mode, off by default, with code from extraction to runtime activation; no unpublished external R&amp;D process or user-installed extension is required. Effectful guards need explicit operator authorization; advisory promotion can be autonomous once opted in. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prospective hypothesis/capability selection is produced by a model outside an individual current-task coding loop, then evaluated and fed back into later operational capabilities. It is not mere text-only memory recall or compaction. Both autonomous and parent-governed decisions have distinct documented roles. - Evidence: src/supervisor/learning/evolution/synthesize.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/runtime.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/registry.rs, src/supervisor/learning/extract.rs, src/session/context.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - External distinction: observations gathered across past sessions and project/role boundaries rather than an in-flight same task&#39;s tool result. - Future / prospective distinction: choose whether a machine-generated reusable skill/guard should persist as available behavior for later tasks. - Adaptation option generated: scope-bounded, verified native skill/guardrail candidate with a registry identity and explicit artifact path. - Path back into current capability / S3: runtime initforsession loads active generated skills and merges validated guardrails; promotion/retirement changes subsequent specialist behavior while gate/plan continue regulating current work. The operator may approve effectful candidates or revert the registry state. - Basis: first-party optional autonomous skill-generation→trial→activation and bounded parent rights. - Confidence: medium-high for source-confirmed pipeline; actual learning benefit not measured. - Caveats: evolution disabled in default config; without activation/grounded lessons or enough verified samples no positive rollout occurs. Distinguish confirmed code path from successful on-device trial. Parent permission for effectful native behaviors is mandatory.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimately constituted ultimate identity and organizational-purpose policymaker beyond scoped user/role constraints within an Octomind session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool misuse, role conflicts and evolved guardrail risks are addressed by operator instructions, bounded security policy and optional supervisor authorizer but do not constitute a separate S5 mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret and resolve top-level organizational identity, constitutional purpose and ultimate contested policy with binding return to S3/S4; not established. - Decision owner: external operator provides objectives/configuration, first-party security controller enforces those constraints, supervisor authorizer may optionally judge specific tool intent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role settings, hard guards, user-intent authorizer, command confirmation, generated-effectful authorization and user approval. - Closure path: a disallowed tool can be blocked, but no separately chartered ultimate organization policy/identity judgment returns to govern system aims; parent S4 guard approval does not create one. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy-as-code and delegated parent permissions are not sufficient to demonstrate a legitimately differentiated S5 above the task supervisor. - Evidence: src/supervisor/authorizer.rs, src/session/guardrails.rs, src/config/mod.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/runtime.rs. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a user or adopting organization might own external S5, but that is not a first-party installed control loop at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ae89fd48a07e9d2be6e67d313d1dfd935d61c95f/assessments/octomind.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ae89fd48a07e9d2be6e67d313d1dfd935d61c95f\">ae89fd4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T17:09:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/echoVic/blade-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Blade Code</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/echoVic/blade-code/tree/30f868411df4f7da095aadb7a2a8ca3310315613\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 30f868411df4f7da095aadb7a2a8ca3310315613\">30f8684 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform software-engineering and automation work through model-selected repository/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation choices, tool/test/browser failures, model/provider variation, context pressure and bounded delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret returned evidence, decide when to delegate or create a team and decide when the assigned operational objective is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Blade Code agent and, for delegated bounded work, first-party subagent/team-member agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/executor, permissions, context/session runtime, provider adapters, task admission, goal state, worktrees and cancellation. - Closure path: user/parent goal → agent chooses direct or delegated coding action → first-party runtime executes → tool/subagent/team evidence returns → agent revises work → completion is accepted when runtime completion gates permit it. - Boundary reachability: ordinary CLI/Web/Headless/ACP runtime uses the same first-party Agent/tool machinery; Task and Team tools are shipped model-callable surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes and constrains actions, but the model chooses open-ended implementation, investigation, delegation and completion decisions. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/agent/Agent.ts; packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/task/task.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: permissions and scheduling can delay/block actions without becoming the owner of the engineering decision itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrent operational teammates by assigning dependencies/ownership and isolating write-capable workstreams while coordinating peer progress. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate task claiming, dependency-order violations, concurrent workers editing overlapping workspace state, and peer work that must be synchronized before downstream tasks proceed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the team members, task partition, dependency graph, initial assignments and whether work benefits from parallel specialization/peer review; use peer messages to adjust coordination as execution unfolds. - Decision owner: the model-backed team lead through TeamCreate/SendMessage, with deterministic task-graph/worktree mechanisms enforcing the selected relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamTaskGraph, atomic claim, automatic unblock, durable peer mailbox, managed worktree isolation for write-capable members and worktree lifecycle/merge machinery. - Closure path: lead identifies separable/interdependent work → creates roles/tasks/dependencies → teammates claim only unblocked work and write-capable members default to isolated worktrees → completion/unblocking/messages update peer execution → downstream units proceed under the adjusted coordination state. - Boundary reachability: TeamCreate, TeamTaskClaim, SendMessage and managed teammate worktrees are shipped first-party runtime tools, not documentation-only concepts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces claims, dependencies and isolation, but the lead agent chooses the substantive organizational partition and dependency structure. - Distinct S1 units: persistent model-backed teammates/subagents that own bounded implementation or review outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent workers can duplicate work, violate ordering, or collide through shared writes; managed worktrees and dependency/claim rules exist specifically to reduce those interactions. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead-selected DAG/assignment plus atomic task claiming, automatic dependency unblocking, durable peer messaging and worktree isolation for writers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: completed tasks unblock dependent work; peer messages enter running teammate turns; task ownership/claim state changes which unit may work next; isolated results are later integrated rather than concurrently overwriting the same checkout. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanisms regulate concrete interaction among simultaneously viable operational workers rather than merely transporting a delegated prompt. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/team/teamTools.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamTaskGraph.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/SubagentWorktreeLifecycle.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: atomic claiming and worktree mechanics are deterministic support; autonomy is credited to the lead&#39;s model-owned organizational decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a live whole-team view and regulate current operational commitments while the multi-agent organization is running. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled or unwanted teammates, changed current priorities, task/member status changes, blocked downstream work and a team that should be stopped after synthesis or failure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the live team/member/task graph, steer a running teammate with a direct message, broadcast a changed instruction, and terminate running teammates by deleting the current team. - Decision owner: the model-backed team lead through TeamStatus, SendMessage and TeamDelete. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSessionStore-backed live member state, durable task graph, TeamMailbox, running-turn message delivery and team/member cancellation. - Closure path: lead inspects TeamStatus/current graph → detects a current execution need → sends targeted/broadcast steering or terminates the team → running teammate/session state changes → later TeamStatus/results reflect the intervention and the lead proceeds accordingly. - Boundary reachability: TeamStatus/SendMessage/TeamDelete are shipped model-callable first-party tools available to the team lead. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: live state storage and cancellation transport are deterministic, but the lead model interprets whole-current state and decides the substantive intervention. - Whole-system current view: TeamStatus returns live member state from AgentSessionStore together with durable task-graph state for the owned team. - Current-control decision scope: current peer steering, task/role supervision through the shared graph, and cancellation of running team commitments when work should stop. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/team/teamTools.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamRuntime.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamMailbox.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic stop/cancel would not be enough alone; the positive claim rests on the combined live whole-team status surface plus targeted running-turn steering and commitment termination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge qualifying implementation work with a fresh read-only verifier whose evidence can block completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect implementation claims, failing tests/types/lint, medium/high-risk defects, race/security issues and author self-verification bias. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently execute allowed verification commands, inspect the authoritative changed-file scope and emit structured PASS/FAIL/PARTIAL findings. - Decision owner: the separately instantiated built-in verification agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh subagent context, read-only tool restrictions, authoritative verification prompt, mutation-revision tracking, bounded retry gate and deterministic completion policy. - Closure path: main agent changes qualifying implementation → runtime requires fresh verification Task → independent verifier gathers direct project/tool evidence and emits verdict → FAIL/PARTIAL forces repair/re-verification; only fresh PASS for current mutation revision permits completion. - Boundary reachability: independentVerification is wired into the shipped main loop and explicitly requires the built-in verification subagent for qualifying changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier model owns the audit judgment from its own evidence path; deterministic runtime code enforces freshness and blocks completion when the verdict is insufficient. - Claim being audited: the main coding agent&#39;s claim that the current non-trivial implementation is correct and ready to finish. - Ordinary reporting path: the author agent&#39;s normal tool results, implementation narrative and self-checks. - Complementary access path: a separate read-only verifier runs project checks and reads changed files directly under an authoritative scope. - Independence boundary: fresh model-backed subagent, no write tools, no nested subagents, tool-evidence-only instructions and no reliance on the author&#39;s claimed test outcome. - Who acts on findings: the main agent must repair reported failures and invoke a new verification run; the runtime completion gate prevents success until the required PASS is fresh. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/agent/loop/independentVerification.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/verification.md; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/builtinVerificationAgent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic code-review skills are not used for this claim; the positive S3 path is the enforced built-in verifier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying future/external condition is placed under a function that develops adaptation options and returns them into current S3 capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auto-memory, compaction-time consolidation, provider health/recovery, web/browser tools, goals, schedules and plugin/skill configuration can affect later work but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory consolidation extracts preferences/conventions/lessons/debugging notes and injects retained project knowledge into later sessions; that is persistence/learning context rather than an evidenced outside-and-then option-development conversation with current S3. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/memory/AutoMemoryManager.ts; packages/cli/src/memory/MemoryConsolidation.ts; packages/cli/src/goals/executionFrontier.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the runtime has substantial persistence and external tooling, but these do not satisfy the Methodology 0.3.6 external-and-prospective option loop without an adaptation owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy conflict is routed to a distinct authoritative S5 owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes, workspace trust, system prompts, tool policy, plugin/MCP security and configuration constrain execution but do not create identity-governance closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Blade Code agents operate within developer/user-authored constraints and do not have authority to redefine the system&#39;s ultimate identity or governing principles. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/config/PermissionChecker.ts; packages/cli/src/security/WorkspaceTrustService.ts; packages/cli/src/prompts/sections.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: operator permissions and configuration are legitimate constraints, not identity/ultimate-policy governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37d2b8f891a1fec144fc3d87e36ec29a6c976a96/assessments/blade-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37d2b8f891a1fec144fc3d87e36ec29a6c976a96\">37d2b8f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T23:52:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MaximeRobeyns/self_improving_coding_agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SICA</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MaximeRobeyns/self_improving_coding_agent/tree/ed8275dca4d3c5dbf77229964351fe9b424797dc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ed8275dca4d3c5dbf77229964351fe9b424797dc\">ed8275d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: First-party agent loops perform model-directed coding/source inspection, sandboxed shell operations, benchmark problem solving and independently executing delegated workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New bug/task descriptions, mutable repository content, tool errors, model output and local experiment conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model selects concrete coding steps and allowed tools after receiving tool outputs or prior subagent results. - Decision owner: Executing CodingAgent/ProblemSolver child model, MainOrchestrator&#39;s bounded solver actions and base agent tool-call continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent classes, tool parser and call graph, executecommand, file and edit tools, sandbox Docker process. - Closure path: Task → model-directed operations → actual tool/command outputs and artifact → subsequent step or returned result, with sandbox limits. - Boundary reachability: Packaged baseagent.agent runs MainOrchestratorAgent, which can create coding/model workers; shipped runner.py invokes that executable in isolated Docker containers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task-specific coding decisions are model-generated rather than only an external benchmark script. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/coder.py; baseagent/src/agents/agentcalling.py; baseagent/src/tools/executecommand.py; runner.py - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent distinct concurrent benchmark-running agent S1 workers from interfering through shared host HTTP ports and shared benchmark result-tracker mutation. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple Python/Docker child coding agents each solve separate benchmark problems through runbenchmarkswithjobqueue and processjob, independently maintaining a coding task loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: If two independently executing benchmark agents bind the same host port 8080/808x, one container fails to start; concurrent writes to the same benchmark JSONL tracker may lose or corrupt result/progress state. - Attenuating coordination relation: runbenchmarkswithjobqueue allocates a unique workerid from bounded idqueue; processjob binds container host port 808{workerid}. Each benchmark uses asyncio.Lock around tracker start/result updates, preventing simultaneous writes to shared provenance. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each worker occupies one reserved ID/port while running its model and returns ID only on exit; subsequent agent work waits for a safe slot or starts on another allocated ID; results can be safely saved and analyzed for next iteration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party ID lease and tracker lock address two named interference modes produced by distinct simultaneously acting model agents; the evidence goes beyond mere task assignment/queue routing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Host port contention and cross-worker result-store writes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allocate/release exclusive host port and serialize benchmark tracker mutations. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic idqueue and per-benchmark asyncio.Lock constructor, not an autonomous S2 mediator model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded maxworkers, unique Docker names, per-problem workdir mounts and benchmark data lock. - Closure path: Several runnable problem jobs → lease workerid/unique host port → parallel model sessions → guarded tracker write → release lease → next job. - Boundary reachability: Default runner.py uses this first-party benchmark job runner during evaluation of each agent iteration; positive S2 is bounded to this concurrent benchmark-agent mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Safe exclusive-resource scheduling is deterministic host-owned (C), not the meta agent&#39;s discretionary choice. - Evidence: runner.py; baseagent/src/benchmarks/base.py - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven executive direction of multiple current subordinate coding workers with shared task progress and corrective redirection based on their execution results. - Whole-system current view: MainOrchestrator owns the top-level task and receives the complete problem and a synthesized live execution tree from CallGraphManager after subordinate calls, including already attempted work/results and current subordinate execution state. - Current-control decision scope: MainOrchestrator selects/decomposes active subagent tasks, assigns complete subtask instructions, scrutinizes their returned evidence, changes the plan, commissions further workers/verification and decides when a coherent current task result is submitted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Subagent failures, incomplete work, duplicate approaches, requirement mismatch and uncertain current progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Discretionary model-controlled reassignment, continuation/stop and verification of the current multi-agent goal; no reliance solely on fixed queue. - Decision owner: First-party MainOrchestratorAgent LLM (A) at parent/current multiunit recursion, separate from subordinate child-model operational work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CallGraphManager, generated execution-tree event, model-driven agent calls, structured task results, cost/time governors. - Closure path: Current top-level intent → orchestrator decomposition → model worker runs → returned results plus direct callgraph operating state → revised plans/delegation and final task acceptance. - Boundary reachability: baseagent/agent.py constructs MainOrchestratorAgent and starts it as the initial executing actor in CLI and benchmark mode; role documented in the agent&#39;s first-party core prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The parent model uses evidence and discretionary judgment to govern concurrent/successive independently thinking subagent units, rather than merely running a predefined list of subtasks. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/mainorchestrator.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/manager.py; baseagent/src/agents/agentcalling.py - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate model-driven runtime monitor checks independent live callgraph/effect events for subagent nonprogress, looping and abnormal execution and can issue corrective notices or terminate faulty child work. - Claim being audited: Operational actors&#39; implicit assertions of continuing useful work and healthy nested execution; notably a child may be silently looping or failing while its parent awaits results. - Ordinary reporting path: Each coding child returns self-authored work and final text to its parent, potentially omitting stalled nested activity. - Complementary access path: An independently launched Overseer model periodically/event-triggeredly reads generateoverseerexecutiontree(CallGraphManager), a first-party event-derived live execution graph, not only the child/parent&#39;s narrative result. This is source-observed execution, although not an independently executed code quality benchmark. - Independence boundary: Overseer is a separate model invocation outside producer and supervising parent tool-delegation turn, with its own model option and timing/event observations. Shared first-party callgraph and provider limits remain; no external independent auditor claim. - Who acts on findings: Overseer itself publishes OVERSEERNOTIFICATION to an implicated agent/parent and calls CallGraphManager.cancelagent when model judge sets forcecancelagent; parent responds via next tool/model decision. It also adapts future monitoring cadence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task nonprogress, recursion loops, stalled/erratic worker execution and hidden operational states. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model independently decides concern/notifiability, which actor to notify, whether to cancel, and whether future checks are time/event-driven. - Decision owner: Separate autonomous Overseer model, not a rule-only timeout checker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EventBus subscriptions, execution-tree renderer, OverseerJudgement schema, LLM check interval, notifications and cancellation path. - Closure path: Real child event and call graph → separate model Overseer diagnosis → notification or direct cancel → parent agent control loop sees issue and can reassign; subsequent oversight resamples. - Boundary reachability: baseagent/agent.py always instantiates and starts Overseer when running packaged agent; its monitoringloop subscribes to tool result/assistant events while root agent executes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit&#39;s judgment and direct cancellation/intervention are separate model decisions using a complementary event-derived operating view; not a producer self-review or passive observability dashboard. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/oversight/overseer.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/reporting.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/manager.py - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: medium-high for wiring, medium for audit effectiveness - Caveats: Overseer is an execution-health complementary audit, not independent code correctness review. It may share model provider and callgraph evidence with producer; cancellation can be mistaken or delayed. ReviewCommittee is separately model-driven design critique but is not credited as direct runtime correctness audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven future-capability adaptation: evaluate externally defined benchmark tasks and past agent-version results, generate and implement software architecture/tool changes for subsequent agent generations. - External distinction: Benchmark tasks and measured grader outcomes originate outside the current coding agent&#39;s self-report; the archive records past failures, costs and utility scores. Published literature/web exploration can inform new feature options in the meta-improvement reasoning workflow. - Future / prospective distinction: The meta agent is tasked to improve the next version&#39;s general coding competence, not just solve the currently evaluated benchmark problem or fix the present code task. - Adaptation option generated: MetaImprovementReasoningStructure directs archive analysis (working and failed features), a model-chosen new feature/strategy, code change proposal, multi-member read-only ReviewCommittee critique, model-directed implementation and planned tests. - Path back into current capability / S3: runner.py evaluates a version, selects a base version by stored benchmark statistics and confidence bound, copies code into next iteration, runs that version&#39;s model-driven improve mode on its own codebase, then rebuilds Docker and runs subsequent version benchmarks; resulting changed tool/prompt/coding policy becomes future subordinate S1 capability used by MainOrchestrator&#39;s current-work organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-term changes in measured coding benchmark performance and repeated feature failures, rather than current tool-loop transient errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses which agent architecture/tool/behavior feature should be changed after comparing archived experience and review committee feedback, with real downstream implementation and retest. - Decision owner: Meta-improvement MainOrchestrator/CodingAgent and archive analysis model in the explicit self-improvement operating mode; version selection/testing is first-party deterministic support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Benchmark registry, archive analyzer with utility/CI, selectbaseagent, independent model-driven ReviewCommittee, meta-improvement reasoning structure, agentchangelog.md, isolated next-agent directory. - Closure path: External benchmark/task outcomes → version-level archive → model diagnosis and novel future improvement option → separate committee critique → actual coding changes to next version → newly launched/evaluated agent iteration and updated archive. - Boundary reachability: Documented python runner.py default cycle executes benchmarks and then runmetaagentbenchmark, which launches baseagent improve mode. That mode integrates meta-improvement reasoning and committee. No external company service is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Future capability choice and proposal/refinement are model-authored, not merely a static benchmark-triggered template or heuristic version selector; first-party script closes deployment/evaluation. - Evidence: runner.py; baseagent/src/tools/reasoningstructures/metaimprovement.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/mainorchestrator.py; baseagent/src/tools/committeedesign.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/archiveexplorer.py - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: medium-high for model-driven improvement cycle, low for empirical net improvement - Caveats: A new iteration is produced even when benchmark gains are not established; selection uses observed statistics but does not prove monotonic improvement, safety or benchmark transfer. No production performance results inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No evidenced legitimate ultimate-purpose/policy authority with identity-level deliberation and binding return across all future agent adaptations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risk of benchmark Goodharting, security tradeoffs, resource spending and deciding which future changes are acceptable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Humans configure objectives/budget, benchmark suite, model credentials and Docker trust. Committee can critique designs but cannot ratify ultimate identity or adjudicate mission change. - Decision owner: External experiment operator and developer-set prompt/policy boundaries; no dedicated legitimate S5 body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ReviewCommittee &#39;taste maker&#39;, testing manager, fixed guards, benchmark score criterion, sandbox/budget limits. - Closure path: Committee reports recommendations and model may refine next feature; no identity-level issue/ultimate authority/legitimate binding policy return at S5 recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A sophisticated self-improvement S4 or design-review committee does not imply governing constitutional/organizational identity authority. - Evidence: baseagent/src/tools/committeedesign.py; runner.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/reviewcommitteemember.py; baseagent/src/config.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3efb20aba48801604f7585275a138d80bcb905ee/assessments/sica.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3efb20aba48801604f7585275a138d80bcb905ee\">3efb20a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T22:28:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Snowflake-Labs/cocoplus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CocoPlus</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Snowflake-Labs/cocoplus/tree/b252a8e7e09da6ca3178000d1fb5f48783dffc13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b252a8e7e09da6ca3178000d1fb5f48783dffc13\">b252a8e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist/persona agents autonomously perform the data-engineering/project transformations defined by the active CocoPlus goal/flow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: requirements, code/data artifacts, tool/Snowflake results, tests, implementation uncertainty and bounded project-state changes within each specialist remit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local implementation/analysis/tool actions needed to complete the assigned stage/iteration outcome. - Decision owner: model-driven CocoPlus specialist/producer agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: personas, Snowflake Coco host, phase/stage contracts, model-tier floors, isolated worktrees, artifacts and tool/policy hooks. - Closure path: specialists produce artifacts/evidence in their bounded stage; outputs are persisted and passed to later stage/review/current-control paths, affecting subsequent project work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic gates/hook policy restrict actions but do not choose the specialist&#39;s substantive solution; the model-driven actor owns local operational judgment. - Evidence: README.md and CocoForge/CocoFlow documentation describe specialist agents executing build/test/review/project stages and autonomous long-running modes. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate dependencies and destructive concurrent interference among specialist/producer S1 units while preserving useful parallelism. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependency-invalid stage starts, concurrent producers touching overlapping file scopes, incomplete work contaminating another agent&#39;s context and oscillation between parallelism levels after failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which approved-plan work can run simultaneously versus must wait, and adapt stage parallelism from current dependency/failure conditions. - Decision owner: model-driven CocoHarvest/Team Lead coordination actor operating under the first-party flow contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency graph/gates, isolated worktrees, disjoint producer filescope, checkpoint artifacts, concurrency/retry budgets and deterministic dispatch guards. - Closure path: the coordination decision changes which stages/producers dispatch, which wait, and the scope they receive; dependency/checkpoint failures and disjoint-scope enforcement prevent unsafe execution until the coordination condition changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dependency/worktree mechanisms enforce safe boundaries, while the agentic harvest/lead path chooses the useful parallelization/coordination response within them. - Evidence: docs/features.html calls CocoHarvest the intelligence behind parallelism and describes dependency-aware waits/isolated worktrees/adaptive stage parallelism; AGENTS.md.template requires concurrent producer scopes to remain disjoint. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: plain phase sequencing is not credited; the positive witness is the concrete dependency/write-contamination disturbance with an agent-owned parallelism decision. - Distinct S1 units: two or more specialist/persona/producer agents executing separate CocoFlow/CocoForge/CocoFleet work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping writes or dependency-invalid parallel stages can contaminate shared state, create conflicting outputs or cause downstream work to start from incomplete prerequisites. - Attenuating coordination relation: CocoHarvest/Team Lead chooses parallel versus waiting/dependency order; disjoint file scopes, isolated worktrees and checkpoint gates enforce that chosen separation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: only admitted stages/producers dispatch, blocked dependencies remain waiting, and failure/regression signals can narrow parallelism before later execution. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism is explicitly tied to preventing cross-agent contamination and dependency conflict, not merely assigning or ordering tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current whole-run commitments, specialist dispatch, iteration/milestone progression, budgets, recovery and live intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failing iterations, budget pressure, stalled stages, specialist output disagreement, quality-gate failure, current run-policy constraints and exceptions requiring developer intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autonomous mode — choose iteration plan/dispatch/synthesis/next milestone response and current recovery within hard bounds; parent mode — clear human-gated stages, steer/stop/resume or resolve a dispute when current-control authority is deliberately reserved to the developer. - Decision owner: autonomous mode — model-driven Team Lead/run coordinator; parent mode — developer/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forge-state.json goal/iteration/phase/budget/team/history, milestone gates, run-policy snapshots, stall detection, retry budgets, AGENTSTOP/STEER, human-gate files and deterministic pre-tool policy hooks. - Closure path: autonomous lead decisions change specialist assignments, iteration state and next-stage progression; parent decisions clear/retain gates, resolve disputes or stop/steer the run, and the persisted state/policy governs subsequent agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Team Lead owns current orchestration within the established run boundary; hard hooks enforce constraints, while explicitly documented human gates/dispute/steering paths preserve a separate parent-owned mode rather than being attributed to the runtime. - Evidence: forge.skill.md assigns plan/dispatch/synthesis/milestone-gate decisions to Team Lead and persists whole-run state; AGENTS.md.template supplies AGENTSTOP/STEER, non-bypassable human gates and run-policy boundaries; docs/features.html supplies recovery/escalation/adaptive stage control. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: static policy enforcement by hooks is not credited as S3 ownership; the mapping depends on Team Lead discretion and explicit returned parent decisions. - Whole-system current view: current forge goal, iteration, phase, budget status, team, history, stage dependencies, evidence/critic status and run-policy snapshot. - Current-control decision scope: iteration plan, specialist dispatch, synthesis, milestone progression, current recovery/parallelism and developer-gated intervention.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge specialist/producer quality claims before milestone/signoff using a structurally separated critic path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer self-approval, rationalization, hidden quality regressions and acceptance of evidence that does not meet stated criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue PASS/REJECT quality judgment from the independent evidence presented to the critic and gate progression accordingly. - Decision owner: model-driven Quality Critic / read-only fleet critic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: critic read-only role, lack of specialist reasoning traces, default-REJECT stance, producer/critic separation, required PASS signoff and milestone/refinement gates. - Closure path: critic verdict enters the milestone/flow state; rejection prevents acceptance/progression and drives another fix iteration/dispute, while PASS permits the governed continuation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critic agent owns the quality judgment; read-only/separation/gating mechanisms preserve independence and enforce its consequences but do not produce the judgment. - Evidence: AGENTS.md.template states that the Quality Critic receives iteration outputs but not specialist reasoning traces, starts from REJECT and cannot be overruled by Team Lead; fleet mode separates producers from read-only critics and can require PASS; forge.skill.md places critic verdict in each milestone gate. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: routine tests/evidence gates are not the S3 witness; the mapping is specifically the independent critic path with complementary output-level access and non-overridable feedback. - Claim being audited: that specialist/producer iteration output meets the stated goal/quality/evidence criteria and is safe to accept for progression. - Ordinary reporting path: specialist/producer returns its output/artifacts into the Team Lead/flow iteration. - Complementary access path: separate Quality Critic/read-only critic receives iteration outputs/evidence without the specialist reasoning trace and evaluates them under its own criteria/default-REJECT stance. - Independence boundary: critic is separated from producer, read-only in fleet mode, lacks producer reasoning traces and cannot be overruled by Team Lead. - Who acts on findings: Team Lead/flow milestone control consumes PASS/REJECT; rejection forces another iteration, withheld signoff or dispute escalation before progression.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: inspect externally changed model capability after an upgrade, determine how that changes the value of existing governance/harness rules and develop evidence-backed adaptation options for future operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a newer model may make standing rules/checks newly redundant, advisory or still necessary, creating risk of stale harness constraints or unsafe premature deletion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge which recurring rule/check should remain load-bearing, degrade to advisory or be proposed as obsolete after model-capability change. - Decision owner: constructor path — CocoPlus supplies the S4-specific model-upgrade hygiene analysis, but the standard path does not autonomously close destructive rule changes; developer composition/authority is still required for returned adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: $hygiene --model-upgrade, evidence collection across sessions/committed artifacts, retrospective/benchmark recommendations and prohibition on silent deletion. - Closure path: hygiene produces evidence-backed keep/advisory/obsolete recommendations intended to change recurring hooks/skills/standing context, but destructive adaptation is proposed rather than automatically applied; downstream developer action must complete the return into present capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent can perform the prospective analysis, yet CocoPlus intentionally withholds autonomous closure over the recurring harness rules; this is therefore a supplied S4 constructor path rather than A. - Evidence: cocohygiene/hygiene.skill.md explicitly defines a model-upgrade audit of governance rules/harness checks and classifies their future status while requiring evidence and proposing rather than silently applying deletions; AGENTS.md.template directs retrospective/hygiene/model benchmarking before changing recurring rules. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic retrospectives/learning are not credited as S4. The positive mapping is narrowly the external model-upgrade path; C reflects incomplete first-party adaptation closure. - External distinction: the capability/behavior of the upgraded external model relative to the assumptions under which current CocoPlus rules were created. - Future / prospective distinction: whether recurring governance/harness checks will remain necessary, become merely advisory or become obsolete for subsequent sessions/runs. - Adaptation option generated: retain the rule, downgrade its standing, replace/adjust it or propose removal when evidence shows it is obsolete. - Path back into current capability / S3: the recommendation targets recurring hooks/skills/standing context used by current runs, but developer action is still needed to apply the proposed structural change; this incomplete closure is why the state is C.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct runtime identity/ultimate-policy function is established at the reviewed project/run recursion beyond developer-authored policies, covenants and safety boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: autonomy covenants, hard stops, run policies, safety rules and human approvals constrain operation, but the evidence does not show a legitimate S5 identity/ultimate-policy conversation with closure separate from those constraints/current-control gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party S5 decision right is established. - Decision owner: none established for a qualifying S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: covenant acceptance, run-policy snapshots, hard safety gates, human gates, policy engine, steering restrictions and persistent project configuration. - Closure path: these controls constrain subsequent operation, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate S5 judgment → returned organizational policy loop is established at the chosen recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither a static covenant/policy nor human approval over ordinary current work is sufficient to establish S5; no autonomous or parent S5 closure is inferred from enforcement. - Evidence: README.md, AGENTS.md.template, forge.skill.md and pre-tool-use.js were inspected for ultimate-policy/identity authority; the evidenced functions are current governance, safety and developer control. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e7e949d44dbe4f440ff6ff73d380893b661b9351/assessments/cocoplus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e7e949d44dbe4f440ff6ff73d380893b661b9351\">e7e949d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T15:07:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Xtra-Computing/MegaAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MegaAgent</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Xtra-Computing/MegaAgent/tree/c2e45ad99d8166db82b8f8516d2bcd722ad8540a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c2e45ad99d8166db82b8f8516d2bcd722ad8540a\">c2e45ad &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute a bounded project contribution through a model-driven local task/tool loop and produce shared artifacts, tests, integration or other project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, collaborator messages, current shared artifacts, execution/test results, local TODO/status, tool failures and changing subtask needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local tool actions, artifact changes, communication, testing/execution steps and whether to recruit subordinate capacity within assigned constraints. - Decision owner: each model-driven Agent operational unit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent queue/thread, memory and relevant-history retrieval, generated role prompt, bounded model rounds, task/TODO files, first-party tools, shared workspace and external model transport. - Closure path: assigned/local task + collaborator/artifact state → agent model decision → tool/message/file action → returned execution/file/peer observation → subsequent model decision → local deliverable/result. - Boundary reachability: root main.py directly instantiates the generated CEO and workers through the first-party Agent class; recursive addagent uses the same runtime. No downstream agent loop has to be supplied to obtain model-driven operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic queue/tool/runtime code transports and enforces actions, but substantive local work, tool selection, content and follow-up are chosen by each running model agent from current evidence. - Evidence: main.py, agent.py, llm.py. - Basis: explicit + structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every generated process is automatically a separate viable recursion; S1 credits task-owning operational agent loops at the declared project level.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate dependency/readiness mismatch and conflicting concurrent mutations among distinct parallel agent S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one unit acting before a required upstream artifact is available; two units observing/writing different versions of a shared artifact; stale overwrites and merge conflicts in the common project workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide how to adjust local timing/work after collaborator readiness feedback and how to reconcile/retry a shared-artifact update after current content/hash or merge-conflict evidence is returned. - Decision owner: the affected model-driven operational agents, distributed across the collaborating S1 units; the Git lock/base-hash/merge machinery detects and enforces collision constraints but does not choose the revised content/work response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generated collaborator identities, asynchronous talk, per-agent TODO/status, shared file visibility, readfile current commit hash, writefile lock/base-hash requirement, Git commit/merge and conflict response. - Closure path: inter-agent dependency or shared-file collision risk → peer readiness message and/or version-aware write attempt → current readiness/content/hash/conflict evidence reaches affected agent → model chooses wait/retry/reconcile/change work → subsequent S1 behavior uses the coordinated state. - Boundary reachability: talk, shared-file read/write and Git conflict handling are standard tools generated for every root-runtime agent; the shipped Grace trace demonstrates the dependency-feedback path without downstream wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a lock alone would be deterministic S2 support only. A is credited because concrete dependency/collision evidence is returned into a model-owned next decision: agents choose to wait, notify collaborators, reread or revise the content/work response. - Evidence: agent.py, llm.py, utils.py, logs/Grace.log. - Basis: structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic messaging/delegation is not the basis; the credited witnesses are concrete dependency timing and shared mutable-artifact collision. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running task-owning agents such as implementation, integration and testing workers. - Inter-S1 disturbance: downstream work can begin before upstream deliverables exist, and parallel agents can act on stale/conflicting versions of shared files. - Attenuating coordination relation: peer readiness/blocker exchange plus hash-aware serialized Git mutation with current-state/conflict feedback. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the receiving model agent waits/resumes/reprioritizes or rereads/reconciles/retries against the returned shared state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: each credited mechanism is tied to a concrete cross-unit instability—dependency readiness or concurrent shared-artifact mutation—and changes the next operational behavior that would otherwise interfere.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the project-wide current portfolio of commitments, priorities, readiness, defects and completion/rework on behalf of the whole organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or blocked workers, changing dependency readiness, integration/testing findings, defects requiring reassignment, project-wide quiescence with unfinished work and apparent completion that may still fail whole-project requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current task allocation/reallocation, testing priorities, corrective owner, additional agent capacity and whether the project should terminate or another work cycle should begin. - Decision owner: the model-driven CEO agent (Bob in the shipped default organization). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worker talk reports, CEO subordinate map, shared project artifacts, per-agent TODO/status, global running/idle monitor, automatic wake-up of unfinished agents and final all-idle completion signal. - Closure path: current whole-project reports/artifacts/global completion state → CEO current-control judgment → talk/addagent/inspection/testing/reassignment decision → affected S1 unit changes work → new reports/artifacts/tests return into CEO control. - Boundary reachability: root main.py always creates the generated CEO, sends it the project-start allocation instruction, monitors every live agent/TODO, and returns apparent-completion state to that same CEO for terminate-versus-reassign judgment. The bundled Bob trace shows live priority and corrective routing in this standard path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the global monitor deterministically observes/wakes and supplies state, but it does not choose the organizational response. The CEO model chooses who should work on what next, which defect/priorities matter and whether to create another corrective cycle. - Evidence: main.py, agent.py, logs/Bob.log, logs/Grace.log. - Basis: structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: initial decomposition alone would not establish S3. The positive mapping rests on the later live worker/test feedback and CEO intervention/reassignment loop plus the whole-organization completion/rework closure. - Whole-system current view: worker progress/blocker/test messages, shared output artifacts and the root monitor&#39;s all-agent idle/TODO state provide the CEO with current project-level distinctions rather than a single-child result only. - Current-control decision scope: current ownership, priorities, testing focus, corrective reassignment, additional staffing and terminate-versus-rework decisions affecting multiple operational units and whole-project completion. - Current-control intervention: Bob redirects Dave after Grace identifies an AI defect, instructs Grace to continue/re-test and can recruit/message workers or restart work after the final completion check. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: talk/reassignment enters the relevant agent queue; corrected artifacts and new tester/integrator reports return through the same first-party organization. - Why this is S3-specific rather than decomposition / routing / static workflow: the credited witness occurs after initial decomposition and changes live commitments in response to current cross-project evidence and defects.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge ordinary implementation/integration claims by inspecting and executing produced artifacts, then return discrepancies into corrective project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: latent functional defects or incomplete behavior that are not revealed by ordinary implementer progress/completion messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine from direct artifact/runtime testing whether a produced behavior passes or exposes a concrete defect and publish that finding into project control. - Decision owner: the distinct model-driven testing agent in the generated organization (Grace in the shipped default run); CEO S3 owns the subsequent corrective allocation rather than the tester silently modifying the implementation claim. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tester role assignment, readfile, execpythonfile/interactive execution, test artifacts/reports, talk to CEO/integrator, shared workspace and CEO reassignment channel. - Closure path: implementer/integrator reports and produced artifacts → separate tester reads/executes/tests operational output → tester identifies discrepancy and reports evidence → CEO routes finding to responsible implementation S1 and orders follow-up/re-test → corrected operation is tested/reported again. - Boundary reachability: the standard root organization-generation path can and in the bundled default run does create a distinct tester; its runtime uses the same first-party tools/message channels and the committed logs show the full finding-to-correction handoff. No external evaluator is imported. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the testing judgment and finding are produced by a separate model agent using direct operational evidence. Deterministic execution/file tools expose reality but do not decide whether the observed behavior satisfies the claim. - Evidence: main.py, llm.py, logs/Grace.log, logs/Bob.log. - Basis: observed bundled trace + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic logs and the CEO&#39;s own routine project monitoring are not credited as S3. The positive witness is the separately assigned tester&#39;s direct artifact/runtime evidence and its corrective return. - Claim being audited: implementation/integration units report that delivered modules and assembled behavior are ready or functioning. - Ordinary reporting path: implementers/integrator send progress/readiness messages to collaborators/CEO and update shared deliverables/status. - Complementary access path: a distinct tester reads the actual produced files and executes targeted behavior/tests rather than accepting those status reports. - Independence boundary: Grace is separately generated/assigned as testing work, not the producer of the AI/integration implementation being challenged; her test observations are reported to the CEO rather than being generated by Dave&#39;s own production loop. - Who acts on findings: Grace reports the hard-AI four-in-a-row failure to Bob; Bob sends the finding to Dave for defensive-logic correction, directs Grace to continue testing, and the later organization requests re-testing/integration feedback. - Why this is S3-specific rather than ordinary review / QA / logging / evaluation: the evidence path materially bypasses ordinary implementer claims, directly probes runtime behavior, finds a contradiction and closes that finding into corrective operational work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop that changes future MegaAgent organizational capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the runtime reacts strongly to current task complexity, current shared artifacts and current test outcomes, but no evidenced function models changing external/future conditions for later organizational adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for selecting and promoting a future capability adaptation. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dynamic agent generation, recursive addagent, memory/history retrieval, current-task testing and task-specific reorganization. - Closure path: current task/complexity → current roster/subtask adaptation → current project execution; no prospective environmental distinction → future adaptation options → selected/promoted later capability → present S3 conversation is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents autonomously change current staffing and work in response to present operational variety, but Profile 0.2.3 explicitly separates current task planning/reaction from S4 future/environment intelligence. - Evidence: README.md, agent.py, config.py. - Basis: structural absence after prospective-adaptation review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dynamically generating hundreds of agents demonstrates current variety amplification/scaling, not by itself future-oriented S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the MegaAgent project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the user project goal, CEO name, model/provider choice, maximum rounds/subordinates and additional prompt constrain current operation, but no identity-level S3–S4 tension or ultimate-policy issue enters an authoritative runtime closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config.initialprompt, additionalprompt, user-edited project goal/provider settings, static limits and CEO role generation. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance of subsequent operation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the CEO is an executive/control role for the current project, but executive naming and ordinary project completion authority are not S5. The user supplies initial purpose/configuration before operation; no parent runtime identity escalation path is shown. - Evidence: config.py, main.py, README.md. - Basis: structural absence after identity/policy review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream deployment could wrap MegaAgent in human/institutional governance, but that would be a different system boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2022cc46ef0c83cc6181ff994e9df4bb826f5d11/assessments/megaagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2022cc46ef0c83cc6181ff994e9df4bb826f5d11\">2022cc4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAGS</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher/tree/9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\">9bb9dfc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist research agents perform the operational scientific work: literature review, proposal development, experiments, manuscript production, and peer review/revision artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended scientific questions, external literature, experiment implementation/execution, result interpretation, and manuscript construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next specialist reasoning/tool/file action needed to produce each role&#39;s research outcome within its SOUL/skill contract. - Decision owner: each first-party builtin OpenAGS Agent executing the corresponding specialist folder/role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL and skill loading, tool engine, memory/session/history, filesystem workspaces, upstream/downstream paths, LiteLLM transport, timeouts, and orchestrator lifecycle. - Closure path: observations/tool results return to the specialist Agent loop; the agent updates role artifacts/status and those outputs become available to downstream agents/coordinator. - Boundary reachability: the repository documents a complete first-party builtin Agent path (Agent.loop) used by the research layer; S1 therefore does not depend on Claude Code/Codex/Cursor/Gemini owning the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party agent engine closes model decision → tool execution → observation → next decision inside the supported builtin mode. - Evidence: builtin-agent execution path, folder-as-agent model, default specialist workflow. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: alternate CLI-agent backends are not used to justify S1 ownership; the credited mode is the self-contained builtin OpenAGS agent engine.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific regulation of a demonstrated inter-S1 conflict/oscillation is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: upstream/downstream file contracts, fixed first-pass sequence, TASKS/STATUS files, agent dispatch, and shared project artifacts organize work but do not by themselves establish an inter-S1 interference witness. - Closure path: workflow outputs feed downstream specialists, but that sequencing/dependency path is not sufficient for S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: named multi-agent roles and communication through files are generic organization/hand-off primitives unless tied to a concrete conflict or oscillation between S1 units and an attenuation loop. - Evidence: file-based agent communication, coordinator first-pass sequence. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: workflow dependency management may prevent some practical clashes, but the required S2-specific disturbance/attenuation witness is not established in the reviewed evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-project current view and decide which specialist work starts, waits, reruns, or is complete. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or failed stages, changing project status, downstream dependency readiness, review-identified weaknesses, and need to redirect current research effort. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next active specialist/task and, after the first pass, choose which research stages must rerun in response to current project evidence. - Decision owner: the root coordinator Agent in Auto Mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: specialist STATUS.md and TASKS.md, workflow/orchestrator process management, timeout/failure handling, fixed initial stage order, project files, and start/wait/all-complete action protocol. - Closure path: coordinator reads current statuses/review findings, emits startagent/wait/allcomplete/needshuman decisions and writes/assigns specialist tasks; the orchestrator launches or withholds subsequent work accordingly. - Boundary reachability: Auto Mode and the root coordinator are part of the default first-party project template and builtin workflow, not an external operator or development-only controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic orchestration executes start/stop/status mechanics, while the coordinator Agent is explicitly instructed to make pipeline decisions and decide what happens next from the whole project state. - Whole-system current view: all specialist status files, project-level memory/key decisions, current stage completion, and review outputs across the research project. - Current-control decision scope: assignment and ordering of current specialist work, whether to wait, whether the project is complete, and which stages to rerun after review. - Evidence: coordinator Auto Mode and role, workflow protocol. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: fixed first-pass sequencing is enforcement/support; S3 credit comes from the coordinator&#39;s evidenced discretionary current-control decisions, especially status-based dispatch and post-review rerun choice.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review the manuscript/research claims using complementary access to manuscript, experiment results, literature notes, and external citation verification, then return actionable findings into project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported claims, weak methodology/experiments, citation hallucination or misrepresentation, missing baselines/limitations, and paper-level weaknesses not caught by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make peer-review judgments, classify major/minor concerns, score review criteria, issue a verdict, and prescribe a revision roadmap. - Decision owner: the distinct review Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated review SOUL/folder, read access to manuscript/experiment/literature artifacts, web search for citation spot-checking, structured review format, and persisted review-report.md. - Closure path: review findings are written under review/reviews/; after the first pass the coordinator reads those weaknesses and decides which stages need to rerun, returning audit findings into changed subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: the reviewer is a default specialist in the standard project template and a standard first-pass stage; its report is explicitly part of the coordinator&#39;s iteration mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer makes substantive adversarial/audit judgments rather than merely applying a deterministic syntax gate; the coordinator, not the reviewer, owns corrective project-control decisions. - Claim being audited: the manuscript&#39;s scientific claims, citations, experimental support, logic, completeness, and reproducibility. - Ordinary reporting path: manuscript output and specialist status/results flow normally into the project filesystem and coordinator. - Complementary access path: the reviewer reads the full manuscript plus experiment report plus literature review and independently web-searches sampled citations; it is instructed to adversarially probe alternative explanations/failure conditions/missing experiments. - Independence boundary: a separate review role/folder with its own SOUL and artifact is outside the manuscript-producing agent&#39;s ordinary production loop, while retaining direct access to underlying experiment/literature evidence. - Who acts on findings: the root coordinator reads review weaknesses and selects which stages to rerun; affected specialists then perform the corrective work. - Evidence: reviewer SOUL, coordinator iteration mode. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider independence is not claimed; organizational independence comes from the distinct review role, separate artifact, complementary evidence surfaces, and explicit findings-to-control return path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense the external scientific landscape through the literature specialist and turn those distinctions into future-facing research proposals/experiment direction consumed by later S1 work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk of pursuing already-solved, poorly grounded, or scientifically irrelevant directions and missing externally established methods/results that should shape the project. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and synthesize externally retrieved papers/field distinctions that should inform the project proposal and later research program. - Decision owner: the literature Agent, with downstream proposal/experiment agents autonomously using the returned research intelligence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paper-search skills/web access, literature notes/memory, fixed upstream paths, shared project filesystem, and coordinator sequencing. - Closure path: literature agent searches/reads external work and writes literature notes; the proposal agent explicitly reads ../literature/notes/ and memory.md; experiment/manuscript stages consume the proposal/literature outputs, changing subsequent project work. - Boundary reachability: literature is a default first-party specialist stage in the project template, and its artifacts are wired as upstream inputs to subsequent specialists in every supported runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party literature Agent decides what external evidence is relevant and synthesizes it; file plumbing only returns that intelligence to current capability. - External distinction: current prior papers, methods, results, and gaps retrieved from outside the project. - Future / prospective distinction: which field gaps/constraints should shape the proposal and experiments the project has not yet executed. - Adaptation option generated: literature-grounded research/proposal directions and constraints for experiment design. - Path back into current capability / S3: persisted literature notes become explicit upstream inputs to proposal and later experiment/manuscript work under coordinator sequencing. - Evidence: folder/agent upstream-downstream architecture, research workflow skill. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project memory and peer review alone are not used to justify S4; the credit rests on external scientific sensing and a closed return path into future project work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the research-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL role definitions, user project brief, templates, skills, provider configuration, and needshuman escalation constrain operation but do not themselves establish ultimate-policy governance. - Closure path: no evidenced identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned operation loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator manages current work but is not shown redefining the project&#39;s ultimate identity/policy; generic human input/escalation is not automatically parent-governed S5. - Evidence: coordinator SOUL, architecture. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can direct a project and the coordinator can request human input, but neither fact alone establishes S5 without a genuine ultimate-policy issue and authoritative return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/openags.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-18T02:45:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aws-solutions/konductor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Konductor</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-18\">2026-11-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aws-solutions/konductor/tree/09c34858ca2e704fb8572c91389a87255b1e1075\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 09c34858ca2e704fb8572c91389a87255b1e1075\">09c3485 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute specialized model-conditioned engineering tasks, such as requirements analysis, architecture, implementation, tests and PR preparation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Diverse user requirements, repository code differences, implementation failures, external tools and test/review feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model-backed specialist chooses concrete code/analysis/tool actions and returns artifacts; the orchestrator chooses a suitable specialist for an operating task. - Decision owner: Installed Konductor k-developer, k-architect, k-researcher and similar registered model-backed agent roles, not the Rust installer or deterministic gate evaluator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent spec registry, explicit delegation briefs, host tools, Git worktrees, SOP state/progress files, build/test scripts and bounded fix-cycle requirements. - Closure path: A specialist performs the assigned job, writes or returns deliverables, and its result enters the orchestrator&#39;s review/repair loop; an accepted artifact advances the subsequent phase or is escalated. - Boundary reachability: Rust installation synthesizes source-shipped agent specs, SOPs and skills for supported Kiro/Claude host modes; Konductor&#39;s packaged system prompt invokes explicitly registered specialists and their SOP contracts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM-backed coding/research decisions are owned by the named roles under installed first-party instructions; the executable host&#39;s generalized cognition and Git tools are external support. - Evidence: agents/konductor.agent-spec.json, agents/k-developer.agent-spec.json, agents/k-architect.agent-spec.json, skills/delegation-protocol/SKILL.md, cli/konductor-rs/src/cli/install.rs, agent-sops/k-full-sdlc.sop.md - Basis: first-party distributed agent role prompts plus installer and runnable SOP invocation contracts -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent two independently writing agent units from overwriting the same source files or creating incompatible feature branch/merge results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Separate agent writers can edit a shared router, schema, registry or working tree concurrently; parallel worktrees isolate files on disk but do not automatically avoid merged-path conflicts or lost work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-backed orchestrator must list per-agent write file sets before parallel dispatch and decide whether to give an overlapping file to one agent or serialize the affected parts; it chooses exact worktree targets and coordinates integration. - Decision owner: Installed Konductor model-backed orchestrator exercising the shipped delegation-protocol before assigning multiple writer specialists, backed by deterministic branch/isolation constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Explicit target worktree in the delegation brief, per-feature worktree/branch options, disjoint-file preflight, sequential dependency handoff, integration of separate commits and post-integration build/test verification. - Closure path: Potential duplicate ownership is resolved before work starts through revised agent-file allocation or sequential execution; approved writers receive distinct targets; integration owner combines outputs and runs tests/review before further delivery. - Boundary reachability: The packaged delegation-protocol expressly governs Konductor&#39;s parallel specialist spawns; the shipped Konductor system prompt requires an explicitly named agent and 8-field brief. Full-SDLC SOP exposes branch-vs-worktree mode and physical per-feature isolation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The S2-specific action is discretionary conflict-aware task/file reassignment by the model-backed orchestrator, not generic job sequencing, a host mailbox or Git branch commands alone. - Evidence: skills/delegation-protocol/SKILL.md, agents/konductor.agent-spec.json, agent-sops/k-full-sdlc.sop.md, agent-sops/k-delegate.sop.md - Basis: explicit first-party collision-specific operating contract and return into writer dispatch - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The pre-dispatch file sets must be reasonably identified by the orchestrator; no actual concurrent-run conflict episode was executed. The standard single-branch mode is sequential, whereas parallel disjoint-writer mode is conditional. - Distinct S1 units: At least two model-backed coding specialists on separate feature slices or worktrees operating on a common project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two writers modifying the same registry/schema/router or both writing the same shared checkout could lose changes or generate a merged source conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: The orchestrator enumerates file ownership before parallel spawn, assigns unique target worktree and overlapping file ownership to one writer, or serializes the collision; dependent tasks wait on upstream handoff. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each writer&#39;s dispatch brief names its actual worktree/file scope and revised timing; resulting artifacts are merged into integration work, tested and reviewed before downstream operations. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The concrete shared-file and overlapping worktree interference is named and resolved through assigned ownership/serial fallback with observable changes to subsequent writers; multi-agent delegation without that remedy would not qualify.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Steer currently active project delivery by assigning specialist commitments, tracking phase status, gating quality, managing bounded retries and escalating blocked work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incomplete phases, specialist output defects, failed build/review, blocked dependencies, parallel task limit exhaustion and critical findings beyond repair budget. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-backed Konductor routes current specialist work, owns the TODO/phase picture and chooses whether to repair, redelegate, wait for human authority, or stop under the 2-strike and review ceilings. - Decision owner: Installed Konductor orchestration agent over the local project delivery cell; humans retain separately reserved approvals of individual steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOP state map, deterministic Rust gateeval and severity thresholds, phase recording, TODO, receipts, specialist reports and human checkpoint prompts. - Closure path: After specialist results, Konductor checks source evidence, updates phase and issues corrective briefs/reassignments or blocks progress; accepted results unlock the next phase and exceptions reach an operator for decision. - Boundary reachability: Source-shipped Konductor system prompt requires TODO and current review ownership, 2-strike routing and full-SDLC phase orchestration; installed SOPs and gate configuration are executable by the host agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Konductor makes the cross-specialist current-control decisions; the Rust gate check and human approval forms constrain actions but do not replace the model&#39;s orchestration judgment. - Evidence: agents/konductor.agent-spec.json, agent-sops/k-full-sdlc.sop.md, agent-sops/k-delegate.sop.md, cli/konductor-rs/src/cli/gateeval.rs, cli/gate-config/sop-orchestration.yml - Basis: first-party installed role decisions plus phase tracking and native gate-enforcement path - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The whole-system scope is one project/task delivery team, not global organization resource management; no separate whole-team parent S3 closure is claimed merely from human checkpoints. - Whole-system current view: Konductor&#39;s required TODO status, per-phase SOP session/checklist, returned specialist results, review gates and current remaining fix cycles across the project/task. - Current-control decision scope: Assignments, rework responsibility, sequence/parallelism, phase priority, reroutes, bounded recovery and escalation of current specialist commitments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge coding/review assertions with real Git diffs and multiple separate evaluation perspectives before release/merge recommendation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Defective code masked by a coder&#39;s self-report or routine review, latent security/schema/data-integrity holes and inaccurate proposed fixes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Three isolated adversarial generator agents (security, integrity, schema), a separate reuse evaluator and a fresh k-architect validator make independently role-separated judgments against the actual source diff, producing a blocking/nonblocking verdict. - Decision owner: Distinct model-backed adversarial reviewers and fresh checker role in packaged k-adversarial-pull-request-review SOP, not the orchestrator who coordinates handoffs nor a deterministic word severity table. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git diff intake, checker literal-diff verification, independent context boundaries, typed findings/severity, review ceilings, SOP result recording and gated phase progression. - Closure path: Independent findings are checked against real changed hunks, translated to critical/important report and returned to orchestrator/implementer for a bounded fix and new review; unresolved important defects block completion and are escalated. - Boundary reachability: k-code-review-workflow directly requires an adversarial pass on its current diff, and k-adversarial-pull-request-review supplies concrete generator and checker role assignments; Konductor registers those SOPs in its distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Substantive review judgment is made by fresh role-separated agents with source access; mechanical gateeval and report aggregation enforce the verdict but do not independently author the audit. - Evidence: agent-sops/k-code-review-workflow.sop.md, agent-sops/k-adversarial-pull-request-review.sop.md, skills/adversarial-code-review/SKILL.md, cli/konductor-rs/src/cli/gateeval.rs, agents/k-architect.agent-spec.json - Basis: explicit shipped multi-review SOP, code/diff exposure and return-to-fix contract - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Source proves designed role/context independence but not empirical correctness of actual provider calls; this assessment is not a separate independent human OpenSiro S3 audit. - Claim being audited: The implementer&#39;s changed code and PR fulfill requirements without significant security, contract or integration defects. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementer says build/tests passed; basic code-review specialist submits first review report. - Complementary access path: Adversarial SOP reads actual full Git diff and source, parallel independent vulnerability lenses, and a fresh different-persona architect checker verifies findings against literal diff. - Independence boundary: The checker never generates its own candidate findings and starts after generator passes; distinct sessions/personas and prohibition on passing one generator&#39;s findings to the others. - Who acts on findings: Konductor orchestrator routes required fixes to the owning developer/specialist, orders fresh checks per review ceilings, and escalates unresolved critical/important findings to the user.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Plausible durable lesson/skill adaptation exists but the first-party environmental foresight and adopted future-capability change loop is unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unexpected external tool/API behavior, repeated implementation failures, changed project conventions and new constraints that could require revised future practices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The Konductor prompt invokes memory/skill reflexes following error recovery and user correction; skill writes are constrained by user and provenance policies. It is unclear whether this amounts to organizational prospective adaptation beyond retaining case facts. - Decision owner: Agent can propose/select capture candidates, while the user may approve workspace skill creation; full decisive ownership of a closed first-party S4 strategic option-to-capability loop is not demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent-memory stores bounded project/user facts, validator checks safe writes, workspace-skills scans and requests confirmation for reusable procedures; subsequent sessions may reload user-authorized data. - Closure path: Past error/user feedback can become a tagged project memory or approved skill and be re-read on a later run. Source does not demonstrate an independent external-and-prospective strategic option and how it materially changes the managed team&#39;s future capability at this organizational recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A persistence/reflection mechanism may inform future work, but may be ordinary context retention; neither automatic S4=A nor parent S4=P is established from a single-correction memory write. - Evidence: agents/konductor.agent-spec.json, skills/persistent-memory/SKILL.md, skills/workspace-skills/SKILL.md, README.md - Basis: first-party packaged memory/skill contracts and embedded reflex rule; insufficient function-specific prospective governance closure -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate organizational purpose, identity or constitutional policy authority with a returned decision is supplied in the installed project orchestration boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Decisions over the organization&#39;s identity, constitutive authority and ultimate governing purpose rather than one engineering task or reusable project convention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Users approve task-specific irreversible operations and deviations; SOPs enforce fixed gates. No first-party legitimate owner is shown changing organizational ultimate policy and returning that decision to all future operations. - Decision owner: None established at the declared first-party Konductor deployment recursion; the project user&#39;s broader institution is external. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Strict phase approval gates, USER DECISIONS IN FORCE action-specific briefer, configured severity policy, local safety controls, optional user-approved memory/skills. - Closure path: Task and phase approvals resume or block work, but no authored constitutional policy decision-and-return loop is present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool permissions, design review, task override and versioned playbooks are not automatically first-party S5 authority. - Evidence: agents/konductor.agent-spec.json, agent-sops/k-full-sdlc.sop.md, skills/delegation-protocol/SKILL.md, cli/konductor-rs/src/cli/gateeval.rs, skills/workspace-skills/SKILL.md - Basis: bounded review of shipped agent rules and runtime gate/parent paths -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/865ab36679280386056e503aa38d5a7cc537535c/assessments/ko/konductor.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 865ab36679280386056e503aa38d5a7cc537535c\">865ab36 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T20:00:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCursor</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor/tree/b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\">b4528f9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: directly interpret, inspect, edit and verify software against a human coding task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code and runtime behavior, incomplete edits, tool errors and feedback from repository/test inspection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose file reads, edits, tool calls, retries, delegated S1 work and final response based on live evidence. - Decision owner: the model-driven coding agent in Agent mode and coding-worker subagents in supported Project/Multitask modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: VS Code tool bridge, file and shell effect adapters, permission/approval policies, session persistence and job display. - Closure path: agent choice → tool execution/permission result → actual file/shell observation → subsequent model decision or answer. - Boundary reachability: the packaged extension directly wires its agent loop and repository tools, no custom embedding host or optional external agent workflow is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the acting model leaves tool wrappers but no task-driven selection/repair; human approves permitted effects but does not choose every next coding action. - Evidence: README.md; loop.ts; index.ts; files.ts; approvalPolicy.ts - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate shared-workspace change collisions and conflicting edits between independently acting delegated code-writing workers in the shipped coordinator/team modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: at least two concurrently active coding children may attempt incompatible modifications or overwrite/revert another worker&#39;s output in their shared checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead model selects nonoverlapping assignments, sends corrective messages when a child flags conflict, and revises affected children rather than blindly accepting conflicting edits. - Decision owner: the lead agent/model in opt-in Project/Multitask configurations; child agents report overlaps; deterministic mailbox and a concurrency cap only execute the decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task schema&#39;s &#39;assign separate files&#39; contract, built-in team protocol&#39;s non-overwrite/flag instruction, stable child IDs, collaborator status, message/follow-up controls. - Closure path: child conflict signal or current roster/result → lead&#39;s scope/plan correction → SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent or new separate assignment → children&#39;s subsequent work; separation also happens prospectively when lead initially assigns distinct files. - Boundary reachability: built-in Project/Multitask prompt paths and child/messaging tools are wired in the standard extension, not derived from a user-created orchestration script. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-scope choice and conflict response are discretionary lead-model decisions; the active-child cap and status store do not own that interference-specific decision. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; teams.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; collaborationState.ts - Basis: explicit mode instructions and structural code-wired feedback - Confidence: medium; a supported decision path, not proof that every conflict is detected or prevented - Caveats: children usually share a workspace; conversation worktrees are optional isolation and not automatic per-child reservations. Ordinary Agent mode does not establish this coordinated path on every turn. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent model/tool-running coding children started via the Task tool, each with its own history, task and executable edit tools. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overwriting/reverting another child&#39;s files or incompatible edits in the shared workspace, explicitly addressed by the shipped Team protocol and Task tool. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead chooses separate file assignments; built-in team members must adapt/flag cross-member conflicts, while root can steer/interrupt/reassign them. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child reports and roster responses enter the lead turn; message/follow-up deliver revised instructions to named still-running or resumed children. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the shipped instructions target a concrete shared-file interference, not only transporting tasks; lead feedback can alter worker scopes after conflict.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the set of active delegated commitments across current Project/Multitask coding operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple live coding children compete for bounded concurrent slots, priorities and dependencies while task results and interruptions change the feasible current plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the complete conversation collaborator roster/status and choose which current worker gets each unit, is steered, followed up or interrupted; revise running commitments in response to results. - Decision owner: the opt-in Project/Multitask lead model; not the fixed max-four gate, job registry, event stream or human authorization engine. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListAgents and stable IDs, activeAgents map, child status/result feedback, SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent/InterruptAgent, root&#39;s intentionally disabled editing tools. - Closure path: whole-session roster/task reports → lead&#39;s portfolio-level assignment/intervention decision → launch/message/interrupt and changed child work → updated statuses/results. - Boundary reachability: the standard Project and Multitask modes explicitly put an LLM lead over all delegated coding tasks with model-facing operational management tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the lead model leaves a cap and worker status API but not a discretionary choice of how to reallocate current work; the supported lead modes are wider than one routine child tool call. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; collaborationState.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; taskState.ts - Basis: structural and explicit first-party coordinator-mode instructions - Confidence: medium; session-scoped, not organizational or multi-deployment management - Caveats: not attributed to ordinary single-worker Agent mode or separate VS Code sessions. Does not mean that all child steps are autonomously centralized. - Whole-system current view: ListAgents exposes all retained conversation collaborators, live status/result and queued-message counts; background job registry tracks running/completed work. - Current-control decision scope: lead-mode model decides present unit assignment, bounded concurrency slot utilization, correction/interrupt, follow-up and completion of delegated commitments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect the implementing agent&#39;s code claims using a separate read-only code-reviewer context and raw repository/diff evidence, then return findings for control of repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer&#39;s self-report or ordinary integrated tool-status check may omit real defects, concurrency problems or unsafe edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate reviewer agent evaluates the actual diff and source context for defects; the implementing parent receives and decides follow-up repair or rejection. - Decision owner: the delegated reviewer model for the complementary judgment, acting only when commissioned, and the lead/implementer model for the downstream corrective choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in read-only code-reviewer and team-code-reviewer roles, child independent history, file-reading/search tools, returned report and parent Task result. - Closure path: parent requests review → separate child reads current repository/diff and forms defect-first findings → report returns to parent history → implementing lead can fix, stop or ask for further check. - Boundary reachability: built-in code-reviewer Task type and built-in team-code-reviewer are available in the shipped extension and run through first-party child-model/tool execution; no user-authored reviewer is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer performs an independent direct-evidence judgment; parent runtime reports/forwards but does not supply the reviewer&#39;s verdict. Ordinary VerificationLedger is excluded from S3 credit. - Evidence: teams.ts; schemas.ts; loop.ts; files.ts; verification.ts - Basis: explicit built-in independent reviewer instructions and source-wired child execution - Confidence: medium-high for opt-in separate-context code review, not for default every-turn checks - Caveats: review and corrective response are conditional on the model/parent invoking the supported reviewer; no claim of Organization S3 governance or fully independent remote operator. - Claim being audited: whether implementing-agent code modifications are correct, safe and maintainable. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer tool outputs, final answer and VerificationLedger test-status summary. - Complementary access path: read-only separately instantiated reviewer model with direct code/diff/repository access and explicit defect-first prompt. - Independence boundary: different child model context/history/role and direct artifacts rather than merely parsing the implementer&#39;s natural-language self-report. - Who acts on findings: calling lead/implementer receives findings through Task result and can select corrective work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external-and-prospective organizational capability-adaptation loop is established in the distributed extension. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external documentation, model/tool changes and potential future feature requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S4-specific adaptive decision/feedback owner identified. - Decision owner: model for present coding and documentation retrieval; user or extension settings for selecting models/skills; neither closes future adaptation of the harness organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: docs discovery/indexing, semantic repository retrieval, reusable personas/instructions, task memory and provider choice. - Closure path: information returns to task context, but no first-party future capability strategy is selected and returned to ongoing operational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: document discovery and planning for current coding work is not a prospective S4 organizational judgment. - Evidence: docsPlanner.ts; loop.ts; README.md; taskState.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy constitutional decision path with binding return is supplied for the coding-session organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate purpose or policy conflicts as distinct from one tool&#39;s allowed/denied action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S5 decisive authority; ordinary permission review authorizes individual actions. - Decision owner: user/host settings and project operators outside the assessed system-in-focus; no evidenced internal/parent constitutional decision chain. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval policy, read-only modes, tool restrictions, workspace instructions and team role prompts. - Closure path: action approvals return allow/deny to ordinary task execution; no identity-level matter → parent ultimate decision → changed governing operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool policy is a safety constraint, not the identity-policy function; an operator&#39;s wider development organization is not imported into this one-session boundary. - Evidence: approvalPolicy.ts; loop.ts; schemas.ts; README.md; teams.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea/assessments/opencursor.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea\">37c1aac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T19:11:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/2118505078xyh1/argentgo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">argentgo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/2118505078xyh1/argentgo/tree/0230ad0cff78aee5c97d182b62f753e2fdb85d71\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0230ad0cff78aee5c97d182b62f753e2fdb85d71\">0230ad0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform coding tasks through repeated model and workspace interactions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain files, failures and action effects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose tools, decide how to use results, and report the outcome. - Decision owner: Model-backed root or child agent carrying an operational task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party tool dispatch, checkpoints and transcript persistence. - Closure path: Tool results enter the conversation and feed subsequent model steps until a terminal output. - Boundary reachability: The ordinary CLI runs this agent loop with the shipped tools, and opted-in subagents use child runs from the same implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model discretion removes the operative coding decisions. - Evidence: internal/agent/loop.go, internal/agent/dispatch.go, internal/agent/dispatch.go, internal/app/appexecute.go. - Basis: Explicit - Confidence: High - Caveats: External providers supply inference. No live calls were made.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent interference between operative children sharing one checkout. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent children can edit the same files, and later work can depend on earlier output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Assign file ownership and decide which children can run in the same wave, then resolve observed conflicts. - Decision owner: The model-backed orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Packaged orchestrator prompt, task dispatch, and separate child context with explicit file scopes. - Closure path: Wave returns expose conflicts and dependencies. The lead changes the following assignment and retries or revises bounded work. - Boundary reachability: A workspace persona activates the first-party orchestrator prompt and task tool in the standard application execute path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses scopes, wave membership and conflict handling. The dispatcher executes these choices. - Evidence: internal/agent/prompts/orchestrator.txt, internal/app/appexecute.go, internal/agent/subagent.go, internal/agent/dispatch.go. - Basis: Explicit - Confidence: High - Caveats: This is prompt-directed regulation without hard worktree isolation. It applies in the enabled persona mode. - Distinct S1 units: Separate subagent runs with concrete operative subtasks and their own run contexts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Simultaneous edits by different child workers can collide in the shared workspace. - Attenuating coordination relation: The packaged lead must put file-overlapping work in successive waves and assign each child concrete file scope. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The lead consumes results and conflicts at the wave boundary and revises or reassigns the following wave. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party wave constraint is targeted to the same-file interference mechanism and requires corrective follow-through.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Control the present workload of one orchestrated request across its worker commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed subtasks and emerging dependencies can invalidate the active execution plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reprioritize or replace current commitments at wave boundaries based on the complete returns. - Decision owner: The lead model in the configured orchestrator mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task scope ledger in the lead conversation, child callbacks, shared execution and bounded dispatch. - Closure path: The lead sees finished workers and failures and changes the next assignment or dispatches a revised retry. - Boundary reachability: The stock prompt is injected by the standard opted-in persona setup and drives the shipped task return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead assesses present work and selects a new trajectory. Static child budgets and limits do not make this choice. - Evidence: internal/agent/prompts/orchestrator.txt, internal/agent/subagent.go, internal/agent/dispatch.go, internal/app/appexecute.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: High - Caveats: The claim covers decision points between work waves. It does not imply fleet-wide control or active cancellation of arbitrary external jobs. - Whole-system current view: Within a bounded request, the leader chooses and observes all synchronous wave commitments, with shared wave results and failure returns in its turn context. - Current-control decision scope: Defer or advance dependent work and decide what to retry, replace or leave out across the remaining operating plan.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Cross-check mutated workspace work through an independently invoked configured verification command and return corrective feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The editing agent&#39;s tool success or narrative may diverge from build or test evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine which direct check supplies the validity judgment for the particular codebase. - Decision owner: The operator or project supplies the independent check and criteria through the supported configuration. First-party runtime owns invocation and return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mutating batches trigger the opt-in verification callback. Workspace checks return exit status and bounded diagnostic evidence. - Closure path: A failure is sent into the editing session as a new correction turn or stops the run after the reflection limit. - Boundary reachability: The monitor path is an explicit first-party post-mutation configuration of the ordinary agent loop. A tool result alone cannot trigger the same feedback without the independent configured check. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate process supplies direct test/build evidence. The operator still selects the evidence and criteria. The standard separate task-verdict mode can judge only reported text unless its prompt requests independent evidence and the necessary tools are composed. - Evidence: internal/app/appexecute.go, internal/app/verifywire.go, internal/agent/verifyloop.go, internal/agent/subagent.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: Medium - Caveats: The verifier can be skipped if it cannot run. No autonomous primary-evidence assessor or hard always-on admission gate is claimed. - Claim being audited: The actual code changes remain valid against an operator-selected build or test criterion. - Ordinary reporting path: The editing run&#39;s tool outcome and its own generated report. - Complementary access path: A separately launched configured workspace check reads or tests the edited tree and supplies independent diagnostics. - Independence boundary: The command runs as its own process after the edited batch and reports the observed result. Domain-specific check selection and strength depend on local configuration. - Who acts on findings: The producing session receives a failure feedback turn. The runtime ends the run when its bounded corrective attempts run out.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Select prospective capability adaptations based on external intelligence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future provider, ecosystem or workload shifts may change suitable capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No in-boundary decision channel from external future evidence to capability adoption was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider probes and fallback, product update checks, runtime recovery and reusable skill authoring. - Closure path: Present provider status can switch the current request. An authorized written skill can be used subsequently. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party skill authoring path persists reusable procedures. The inspected path does not establish prospective external intelligence or an environmental adaptation decision over the whole assessed system. - Evidence: internal/agent/skillauthor.go, internal/agent/fallback.go, internal/provider/healthprobe.go, docs/USERGUIDE.md. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Set which standing operator and project instructions may govern the local coding organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Workspace-provided instructions can alter the standing operational mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select the trusted project instruction boundary and supply standing instructions. - Decision owner: The local operator authorizing project resources for the run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project trust setting, resource filtering and system prompt assembly. Enabled child persona instructions can be inherited inside the team. - Closure path: The permitted instruction sources enter the model system prompt and inherited instruction snapshot for subsequent operations. - Boundary reachability: The shipped CLI exposes project trust, and application construction applies it when including instruction resources in the system prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator controls which boundary can supply governing project instructions. The model carries the applied mandate. - Evidence: internal/app/trustenv.go, internal/app/appprompt.go, internal/memory/boundary.go, internal/cli/flagsusage.go, internal/agent/subagent.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: Medium - Caveats: Tool permission choices are narrower than this evidence. The S5 claim is confined to owner-governed standing instruction authority in this deployment. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whose standing instructions can define the local agent&#39;s operative mandate at the workspace boundary. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The operator supplies standing instructions and authorizes the project-local instruction boundary. - Return-to-operation path: Resource filtering applies the decision when constructing the system prompt and worker instruction snapshot, which govern subsequent model actions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8a19535bf6769126b18a7b89f9505e29c5b58323/assessments/ar/argentgo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8a19535bf6769126b18a7b89f9505e29c5b58323\">8a19535 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T19:00:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aswin-kevin/orchestrix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orchestrix</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aswin-kevin/orchestrix/tree/34447e81f54a605cb780d534d8557573add273a3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 34447e81f54a605cb780d534d8557573add273a3\">34447e8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Deliver operator or host tasks through a model-driven tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain work inputs and tool outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and revise tool actions and end the task. - Decision owner: The run&#39;s model-backed agent, including coordinator workers executing operative subtasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool routing, permission enforcement, events and checkpointing. - Closure path: Tool results return to the transcript and directly condition subsequent model decisions. - Boundary reachability: Both the CLI and SDK run the first-party loop. The coordinator starts workers through the included subagent and async implementations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime supplies model and tools, while the model chooses how to carry out the task. - Evidence: agent/loop.go, agent/schedule.go, middleware/subagents/launcher.go. - Basis: Explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference among worker assignments and incompatible worker outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel agents do not share the coordinator&#39;s context, and their outputs can overlap or contradict each other. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Partition independent assignments and revise work against conflict found in returned outputs. - Decision owner: The model-backed coordinator of the active team. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Default coordination prompt, bounded async slots, task/results registry and live task updates. - Closure path: The coordinator checks gaps or contradictions, revises worker instructions or sends follow-up work, then consumes the revised result. - Boundary reachability: The first-party coordinator constructor installs the default prompt and routes its model to the shipped task tools. Hosts supply the roster, not the coordination callback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The stock model supervisor owns the content of the assignments and how to resolve contradictions. A concurrency cap by itself does neither. - Evidence: coordinator/prompt.go, coordinator/coordinator.go, middleware/async/tools.go, middleware/async/notify.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: Medium - Caveats: This is prompt-mediated regulation in the documented multi-worker coordinator mode. It does not serialize worker edits to a shared backend. - Distinct S1 units: Separately launched worker model runs with their own task context. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping scopes or incompatible results can prevent the assigned workers from jointly delivering the requested output. - Attenuating coordination relation: The coordinator assigns independent scopes before parallel execution and uses results and contradiction checks to revise bounded worker assignments. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Updated instructions enter a running worker before its next step or become a new worker task, with the revised result returned to the coordinator. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The packaged prompt binds assignment partition and returned contradictions to a corrective worker step. The generic task tools alone do not establish the relationship.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current work commitments and worker capacity for the whole request-scoped team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New dependencies, unnecessary running work and incomplete worker commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose whether to revise, cancel or start worker commitments across the current team. - Decision owner: The model-backed coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Thread-scoped task registry, list/check/update/cancel tools and before-stop progress reminder. - Closure path: Status and completion update the lead&#39;s current view. Cancel stops a selected run and returns its disposition; updates change subsequent worker steps. - Boundary reachability: The shipped coordinator composes the same async implementation that exposes the current task list and interventions to the model. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead exercises scope and continuation discretion with the runtime enforcing the selected action. - Evidence: coordinator/prompt.go, middleware/async/prompt.go, middleware/async/tools.go, middleware/async/records.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: High - Caveats: The current view is confined to the assigned coordinator thread. No claim is made for unrelated top-level runs or a distinct whole-team human supervisory mode. - Whole-system current view: Within the assessed team, listtasks refreshes the full thread-scoped background task set. The lead also holds its synchronous work and result history. - Current-control decision scope: Change current commitments through start, update and cancel over the workers assigned to the request, with observed status and outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independently challenge operational claims through complementary evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A worker or lead may report an answer unsupported by actual work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No packaged audit-specific primary-evidence judgment path was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The coordinator reads reported worker results. The rubric checks the final answer against criteria and can trigger revision. - Closure path: Rubric feedback returns to the producing run as an answer revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The standard rubric sees task, criteria and answer. A custom grader or read-only reviewer can add access by supplying external policy and implementation. - Evidence: middleware/rubric/rubric.go, middleware/rubric/grader.go, coordinator/prompt.go, README.md. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Use external intelligence to choose a prospective capability adaptation for the team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future changes in models, tools, or workload can render the present capability mix unsuitable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped future-directed adaptation decision was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider fallback, model profiles, memory, and web tools support configured execution or task work. - Closure path: Fallback and model-specific profiles adjust current model use; memory can inform future tasks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Built-in profiles match configured models and fallback handles failures. Neither supplies a loop from external evidence and anticipated conditions to an adaptation choice. - Evidence: fallback.go, middleware/profiles/profiles.go, README.md. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Define the standing mandate and identity for the operator-launched agent or its configured SDK team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Possible mandates and role boundaries conflict with the operator&#39;s purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select the system mandate under which the top-level model and coordinator are commissioned. - Decision owner: The CLI operator or responsible parent application owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The CLI reads the operator&#39;s system prompt file and passes it to the agent constructor. The SDK coordinator accepts a host-owned prompt and worker roles. - Closure path: At runtime the loaded mandate is assembled into model requests and governs subsequent turns and how the lead commissions workers. - Boundary reachability: orx exposes the operator-owned mandate file directly. A host can also instantiate the first-party coordinator with its own governing prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent receives the mandate and works under it. The operator or parent owner chooses the standing purpose before commissioning the run. - Evidence: cmd/orx/main.go, coordinator/coordinator.go, options.go, agent/loop.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: Medium - Caveats: Per-tool approval does not establish S5. The positive claim concerns the first-party channel for assigning the enduring agent or team role. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which standing role and purpose the commissioned agent or team serves across its user work. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The launching operator or accountable host sets the mandate. No distinct agent-owned ultimate policy mechanism was established. - Return-to-operation path: The selected role is loaded into the constructed agent and presented to the model on each run. The model&#39;s decisions then execute under that mandate.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6607cd88a5d0d006b15c8fc8a9f865e40ba12801/assessments/or/orchestrix.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6607cd88a5d0d006b15c8fc8a9f865e40ba12801\">6607cd8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-19T21:45:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/morphllm/PenHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PenHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-19\">2026-11-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/morphllm/PenHarness/tree/83c540fa9fe9f65b75d949b44e9199f02eb2bea6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 83c540fa9fe9f65b75d949b44e9199f02eb2bea6\">83c540f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce evidence-graded authorized security investigation findings and reports through model-led task cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown target behavior/source, false hypotheses, failures and evidence that changes an investigation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next scoped investigation action, falsify a hypothesis and report a supported outcome. - Decision owner: configured lead/lane/worker models in the first-party installed composition; opencode supplies the external execution engine. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: opencode HTTP/CLI transport, packaged roles/skills/provider config, fresh sessions, fleet scripts, file artifacts and idle continuation. - Closure path: worker actions produce evidence/files; harvest and mailbox reports reach the lead, which chooses new task cells, correction or termination. - Boundary reachability: default opencode.json binds supplied skills/model, the plugin resumes prepared root sessions, and fleet bootstrap copies AGENTS/skills/plugin/config into workers before native fresh-session prompts. This is a shipped composition, not a claim about all opencode internals. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the models choose task-dependent investigations and evidence disposition; HTTP dispatch, JSON parsing and repeat ticks transport their choices. - Evidence: default provider/skill binding, installed lead doctrine, hunt workflow, idle continuation plugin, fleet lifecycle/roles/harvest, worker provider configuration - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: source-only assessment of the supplied workflow. No target, model, cloud fleet, credential or security test was used; prompt-based control is not a security-enforcement guarantee.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate overlapping investigation ownership, duplicate work and conflicting central-ledger writes among work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel lanes/workers can reopen already falsified hypotheses, duplicate probes and contend over shared findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose disjoint task ownership and revise/deduplicate proposed work against the shared proven/dead state before dispatch. - Decision owner: installed lead model and worker/lane models following the packaged coordination role; fixed queues/parser support those decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: one-topic ownership, lead-only central merge, per-worker artifacts, shared dead-end ledger, fresh-session identity, optional flock-backed task dequeue and project-local plugin filtering. - Closure path: workers/lanes inspect existing results before work; proposals and findings return through harvest/mailbox; lead deduplication and new disjoint dispatch change subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: lead doctrine, worker/lane prompts and bootstrap supply the specific anti-duplication/ownership loop; optional queue scripts add mechanical exclusive task claims. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses topic/ownership changes from actual shared evidence. Queue locks, single merge writer and session filters do not themselves own that choice; delegation alone is not the witness. - Evidence: installed lead doctrine, hunt workflow, fleet lifecycle/roles/harvest, queue worker, atomic queue append, idle continuation plugin - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: prose coordination and lead discipline are not a global atomic reservation guarantee. Main HTTP dispatch does not implement the optional shell queue; JSONL append/harvest are not certified race-free.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current hunt commitments and fleet capacity from collective health/progress/evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: idle/unhealthy workers, saturated lanes, changing priorities and continuing cloud cost across the active engagement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base: choose capacity, lane priorities, dispatch/reseed/steer and teardown; parent: inspect fleet status and exercise the same documented operator resource/commitment commands. - Decision owner: base: installed lead model. Parent mode: authorized engagement operator holding the local fleet controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fleet-state.json, status health/session/finding summaries, proposal/mailbox return, up/lane/dispatch/steer/harvest/down and TTL. - Closure path: lead loop reads current ledger/status/harvest and chooses further allocation/intervention; up/down actually provision/destroy workers, steer changes a live lane&#39;s next turn. Operator commands return durable fleet state/status and change active work. - Boundary reachability: the idle plugin explicitly asks the installed lead to inspect current state and select parallel next actions; fleet.py binds those choices to native provider/session actions. README documents manual CLI operation as another supported control mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model/operator owns resource/priority choices. TTL, round-robin placement, health probes and destruction APIs execute/enforce them; neither mere initial task decomposition nor a machine named lead establishes S3. - Evidence: idle continuation plugin, installed lead doctrine, hunt workflow, fleet lifecycle/roles/harvest, supported deployment/manual CLI - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A refers to the admitted lead composition, not an autonomous cloud account authority or externally hosted agent internals. P is local engagement operation, not project-wide governance.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: a concrete independent-verification instruction exists, but complementary audit ownership/closure cannot be reconstructed unambiguously. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a composer&#39;s proposed chain or reported evidence can be unsupported by the underlying observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: which separate actor owns a complementary claim-relative audit, rather than ordinary production proof/control generation, is unresolved. - Decision owner: not established for S3; lead, composers and workers are named but no distinct verifier authority is bound. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh worker sessions, raw proof/control artifacts, candidate queue and central ledger admission. - Closure path: workflow says composer output must not enter the ledger until independently verified and can become fleet queue work; source does not specify the verifying actor/evidence-selection independence sufficiently to establish positive audit or bounded absence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary worker controls and outward audits of the target are S1. The explicit independent composer-verification path is specific enough to prevent simply publishing absence, but generic fresh-session dispatch is not sufficient C/A evidence. - Evidence: hunt workflow, fleet lifecycle/roles/harvest, installed lead doctrine - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: No live trace is required by this assessment as a new threshold. The gap is source-level role/claim/independence/return interpretation of the shipped independent-verification step; separate contexts alone do not settle it.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied prospective adaptation loop for the harness&#39;s future organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new target/source/advisory facts alter the current investigation plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no harness-capability option selection and prospective adoption return established. - Decision owner: models choose investigation tasks/lanes; methodology/configuration changes remain developer/operator inputs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: external source reading, advisory lookup, current lane invention, evidence ledger/dead ends and fixed skill/config files. - Closure path: external facts grow the next hunt actions/chains, the primary product outcome; no first-party reusable capability/strategy evaluation and adoption loop alters the harness for future environments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external reconnaissance and discovering new investigation branches are S1, not automatically S4; a skill authored from a past engagement is historical development evidence. - Evidence: hunt workflow, idle continuation plugin, installed lead doctrine, fleet lifecycle/roles/harvest, hunt state layout - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the target&#39;s possible future risks are part of the investigation product; they do not establish adaptation of this harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication and governing return established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: engagement scope, authorized targets/credentials, fixed red lines and publication permission constrain work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no explicit ultimate-policy issue-resolution path, as distinct from missing task authorization or credentials. - Decision owner: user writes initial scope and approves publication; static safety/mission rules come from the supplied role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scope/brief files, fixed red list, model instructions and plugin readiness/pause/blocked notes. - Closure path: missing scope/credentials causes planning/stop/notes; supplying task scope or ordinary permission does not implement an identity-policy dispute resolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a written authorization file and final human permission are not sufficient S5 evidence. The plugin checks readiness, not legitimate policy authority. - Evidence: engagement boundary template, installed lead doctrine, hunt workflow, idle continuation plugin, hunt state layout - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: scope controls here are largely instruction-based, not a claim of comprehensive enforcement. No external target permission is inferred or exercised by this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c2e43bf4750841277b3e58d8ba80314121734764/assessments/pe/penharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c2e43bf4750841277b3e58d8ba80314121734764\">c2e43bf &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-08T15:26:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dhoelzer/AgentOrchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orchestrator Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-08\">2026-11-08</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dhoelzer/AgentOrchestrator/tree/33fce2cc0d0b3dd4dcd0e7267391b45a2d4edded\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 33fce2cc0d0b3dd4dcd0e7267391b45a2d4edded\">33fce2c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialists perform model-chosen work on project workspace files, commands and tracked deliverables; the root orchestrator is also a model-operated agent, but its regulatory decisions are credited under S2/S3 rather than double-counted as production operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concrete implementation/test/file problems and tool results encountered while completing assigned cards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the specialist model chooses tool actions and reports completion/partial/blocking evidence inside its bounded assignment. - Decision owner: model-backed specialist agent, through OpenHands SDK conversation and first-party tool wiring. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: terminal/file-editor/task-tracker SDK tools, project sandbox, concurrency/turn ceilings, message and event ledger. - Closure path: the specialist changes real project files and returns results to its conversation/parent; the board captures execution/review state, which triggers further activity. - Boundary reachability: the shipped root main.py constructs the runtime and orchestrator; RunAgentExecutor calls dispatch, which passes tools to AgentRuntime.run and records subsequent outcomes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action selection resides with the model-backed specialist; deterministic scheduler and SDK executor transport/enforce choices but do not pick the coding actions themselves. - Evidence: worker and orchestrator tool registry, runtime dispatch, SDK run. - Basis: structural source, supported startup and mocked tool/board tests. - Confidence: high for an installed agent-action path; no credentialed external-provider success run is claimed. - Caveats: OpenHands SDK and external LLM are required; their internal control planes are outside the first-party assessment boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: keep independently operating specialist tasks from simultaneously interfering on shared project files and blocked dependencies. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two otherwise parallel workers touching the same file or a downstream worker starting against unfinished upstream output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root model decides task separation/dependency edges and can re-plan task relationships from each fresh board/attention briefing; the scheduler subsequently enforces the chosen edges mechanically. - Decision owner: root orchestrator agent for the conflict-prevention plan; deterministic scheduler for execution of that model-authored plan. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependson task fields, sequential readiness, project-level slot cap, task statuses, generated briefings and tool-mediated updates. - Closure path: orchestrator chooses dependencies for overlapping work; scheduler refuses to start a dependent task until all predecessor cards are done; completion produces an unblock and new agent dispatch, while blocker/rework feedback allows the next orchestrator cycle to change the plan. - Boundary reachability: the root system prompt explicitly commands dependson when parallel tasks would touch the same files, CreateTaskExecutor stores model-chosen edges, and the shipping scheduler checks them on every sweep. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: conflict-sensitive choice of edges is a model tool call, not an authored static DAG. The runtime&#39;s bare dependency check is not independently S2=A, and evidence does not establish automatic real-time file-lock conflict detection. - Distinct S1 units: separately created project specialists, with each one owning a model/file workspace execution stretch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: named shared-file collision between parallel task workers, together with downstream use of unfinished upstream files. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-authored dependson serializes the interfering work while leaving unrelated cards eligible for concurrent execution. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: predecessor done releases a card to ready, next scheduler dispatch injects task/dependency notes and prior review feedback; root receives current board statuses for re-planning. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the supplied orchestrator prompt ties dependency choice to a particular inter-worker file collision, and the dependency gate actively controls conflicting worker start; generic task order alone would not support this claim. - Evidence: root planning rule, task edges/edit, dependency scheduler, dependency test. - Basis: explicit operating prompt, source-enforced dependency return, local stub tests. - Confidence: medium: the intended shared-file interference is explicit, but live model identification of correct dependency edges was not observed. - Caveats: no actual file-overlap detector or automatic cross-task lock. A model that omits needed edges can still cause collision; mere slot caps are not credited as the agent-owned S2 decision.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-project current board and regulate allocations, staffing, blocked recovery, review and delivery decisions across worker cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unstaffed work, spent budgets, project stalls, failed models, review/rework queues and imbalanced worker/endpoint capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root model chooses task decomposition, worker assignment, budget extension versus reconfiguration/splitting, current plan edits, reviewer dispatch and explicit delivery signal from new board feedback. - Decision owner: the continuously reused root orchestrator model/agent; human edits, pause and config controls impose constraints and may override but do not constitute the credited autonomous path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted Store board, detect() attention, cyclebriefing, createtask, assigntask, extendagent, reconfigureagent, retireagent, config reload, slot gate and deterministic Scheduler.sweep. - Closure path: issue/change/block appears in Store; the next root cycle receives a cross-card and capacity briefing, issues a chosen mutation through board tools, and tasks are dispatched/reviewed/blocked accordingly; later results return to that same Store and next-cycle decisions. - Boundary reachability: main.py launches the never-ending loop; runcycle checks live attention, runprojectcycle uses root SDK model and passes the root board tools, and scheduler runs both before and independently of the root cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: staffing and recovery choices disappear when the root model is removed. Automatic capacity accounting and status transition are supporting controller mechanics, not the entire S3 claim. - Whole-system current view: per-project board, dependencies, agents, blockers, reviewer status, cost/capacity and user messages in fresh root briefings, across the managed specialist S1 cells. - Current-control decision scope: project portfolio task and worker commitments, resourcing, rework direction and delivery timing; no claim that the model autonomously rewrites system-level policy/LLM infrastructure. - Evidence: attention/blocked detection, cycle and project control, orchestrator authority tools, root discipline. - Basis: shipped loop, named board decisions and tests of blocking/dispatch. - Confidence: high for architectural agent-owned project regulation. - Caveats: global cross-project cycle is sequential per project; no separate autonomous cross-domain S3 beyond the installed project portfolio is inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: a distinct reviewer agent independently inspects actual files/testable results against task acceptance criteria and returns a corrective verdict on worker output. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous implementation or unsupported worker claims surviving routine completion reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in a configured reviewer mode, a separate model reviewer decides approved versus changesrequested after reading files; RecordReviewExecutor translates that judgment into done or rework and the root re-dispatches on corrective feedback. - Decision owner: a separate model reviewer when explicitly equipped with the recordreview tool; the orchestrator remains parent control of staffing/response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct reviewer task link, reviewer assignment prompt, read-file/terminal SDK tools, reconfigureagent, review rounds, feedback persistence and rework board transitions. - Closure path: worker finishes to review; parent creates/attaches reviewer; reviewer opens independent file read, checks criteria and records verdict; failing work moves to rework with review feedback in the next worker run. - Boundary reachability: default shipping tool registry permits a root to reconfigure a newly created reviewer with recordreview, then dispatch runagent(review=true); the tool writes verdict and rework state. However CreateSubAgentExecutor validates action.tools but fails to assign it to AgentSpec.toolsoverride, while the default worker tool set omits recordreview. The advertised one-step reviewer creation does not itself close a direct verdict. This is a composable, not reliably default mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct reviewer model rather than creator&#39;s same tool state can inspect actual files and choose a verdict; it needs explicit tool wiring, and default root self-inspection alone is not counted as independent S3. - Claim being audited: a worker&#39;s claim that named project deliverables satisfy task-specific acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: worker&#39;s reporttoparent and normal task completion/summary, which are not themselves independent proof. - Complementary access path: reviewer readfile/terminal reads workspace artifacts and may run tests independently from worker&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: different child agent/conversation and reviewer pointer from worker&#39;s assignedagentid; both share first-party project files, so no stronger sandbox/process isolation is inferred. - Who acts on findings: reviewer verdict switches board done/rework, with orchestrator/scheduler returning failure feedback to the next worker stretch. - Evidence: reviewer prompts, creation tool omission, reconfigure tool/registry, review submission, reviewer scheduler. - Basis: source-level executable mode and mocked board tests, no live reviewer provider run. - Confidence: medium for the explicitly equipped mode; low for author claims of out-of-box automatic reviewer finalization. - Caveats: reviewer creation&#39;s tools argument is presently a no-op at the pin, and reviewerprompt is defined but not automatically selected by CreateSubAgentExecutor. Default prompts alone must not be treated as verified independent adjudication.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate agent-owned environmental scanning, future-option generation and implemented organizational adaptation loop was identified in the installed distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential future tool/platform/model change would require a prospective response, but the observed scheduler chiefly handles current throughput and present user cards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for autonomous outside/then strategy; endpoint selection and automatic timeouts are current deterministic transport tuning. - Decision owner: user changes configured LLM servers and task goals; root agent replans current work but is explicitly told not to change harness configuration itself. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config reload, model endpoint router health signals, new user-input attention, arbitrary specialist prompts. - Closure path: no first-party agent-origin external trend/forecast option is chosen and carried into a durable future capability or strategy change. A user-set configuration may reload but is not such a loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: endpoint tuning and iterative project replanning are S1/S3 current-control support, not evidence of an independently closed S4 function. - Evidence: config reload, do not reconfigure rule, current-attention routing. - Basis: shipped model action inventory, operating prompt and selected state/endpoint paths. - Confidence: medium-high within specified first-party runtime modes. - Caveats: this is not a claim that human maintainers cannot plan future versions outside the harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate first-party ultimate identity/constitutional policy authority is exposed as a separately closed S5 decision process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing portfolio purposes or permanent policy contradictions would require legitimate identity-level arbitration, but observed choices are task/agent instructions and runtime configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced beyond task-level human direction and root-controlled specialist prompt/mission changes. - Decision owner: static installed root ORCHESTRATORSYSTEMPROMPT and the human software owner define core identity; the model can create/reconfigure subagent instructions but not set the harness&#39;s own ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen system prompt, config fields, project scope, human UI pause/manual delivery, per-agent systemprompt overrides. - Closure path: no first-party policy dispute adjudicator with changed identity fed back into the root runtime; changes to child missions remain subordinate S3 control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a root&#39;s ability to author a new worker prompt is delegation/operational policy, not legitimate authority to rewrite its own constitutional identity; static operator settings do not imply a distinct parent S5 mode. - Evidence: fixed root identity, root spec creation, child prompts/reconfiguration. - Basis: first-party identity/prompt tooling search and source inspection. - Confidence: medium-high for absence in the supported runtime boundary. - Caveats: external repository governance by maintainers is not part of the installed agent application assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0a07f100b820ab800e685f36a8047b6cfc54d18d/assessments/or/orchestrator-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0a07f100b820ab800e685f36a8047b6cfc54d18d\">0a07f10 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T17:38:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ai-timon/timon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Timon</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ai-timon/timon/tree/15794bf8db23cb620a40645f273c9b18d7a4b030\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 15794bf8db23cb620a40645f273c9b18d7a4b030\">15794bf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: project worker missions turn assigned programming, research, maintenance and review tasks into outputs in their working environments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository/task contents, errors, uncertain steps, input from other workers and user corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task actions, edits, next tool calls or a completed/blocked response within the role&#39;s constraints. - Decision owner: the configured model-backed coder/researcher/etc. actor launched by Timon&#39;s installed agent composition; external CLI engines supply model execution but do not independently define Timon&#39;s roles, task commitments or orchestration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role templates, backend launch, process fence, tool preauthorization, mission persistence, per-task worktrees and task status API. - Closure path: the role receives task context, executes through the CLI process, reports the outcome via timon task done/block and the dispatcher/wake loop returns that outcome to the orchestrator and subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: standard onboarding/materialization creates complete project agents; ordinary dispatcher and daemon process adapters launch those configured roles without a user implementing a new agent harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed role leaves task routing and enforcement but not the context-sensitive choice of edits, research or substantive review judgment; removing Timon leaves provider CLIs but not this installed team workflow. - Evidence: agent role prompts and seeding; dispatcher routes to role missions; mission/backend process engine; README installation and agent-team boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the configured team; limited to integrations where an external CLI has actually been installed and authenticated. - Caveats: this does not imply model-provider control or verified real-task performance; external CLI/model execution is a mandatory dependency of agent-owned task discretion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concrete interference between simultaneously active code work cells and between a code/review/merge chain and tasks that depend on its integrated output. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent writers sharing a Git base could overwrite/conflict; a dependent coder could run on a branch missing a predecessor&#39;s unmerged changes; multiple review tasks could evaluate conflicting iterations. - Distinct S1 units: separate dispatched coder tasks, review missions and merger missions, which work on distinct task identities and associated Git branches. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping base updates and dependency races between a completed code task and its outstanding review/merge; explicit source comments describe stale-base and duplicate-review failure modes. - Attenuating coordination relation: task-specific worktree/branch creation; dependency gates wait for review pass and merge completion; review-chain single-flight and iteration linkage; worktree base refresh with explicit conflict outcomes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a gated dependent remains waiting instead of executing on the wrong base; review change requests create a next coder iteration; base-refresh conflicts return an explicit path for the acting agent. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: worktree isolation, gated integration and conflicting-iteration control directly address identified concurrent-writing and stale-evidence disturbance, not just a message bus or queue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: set/revise the inter-work-cell isolation and dependency/merge coordination response, distinct from choosing each worker&#39;s task. - Decision owner: Timon&#39;s first-party developer-encoded coordination primitive in the shipped runtime. A model-backed agent does not demonstrably exercise autonomous discretion over revising the conflict-control policy across all concurrent cells. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktree manager, commit/dependency metadata, merge gate, caps and event bus. - Closure path: dispatch checks branch/dependency state and requeues or admits a task; workers then operate only when the relevant integration state can support their next actions. - Boundary reachability: dispatcher imports createWorktree, evaluateDeps and refreshWorktreeBase in the shipped daemon; no third-party coordinator must be authored first. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination relation is real and function-specific but fixed in source and enforced deterministically. Task/agent autonomy does not automatically transfer ownership of these S2 choices. - Evidence: worktree setup and refresh; merge/dependency integration gates; dispatcher and concurrency; code/review/merge chain deduplication. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: C is limited to this specifically evidenced source-native S2 path, not a claim that every cross-project team conflict has an autonomous negotiator.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: control current whole-series/project commitments by breaking work into role-assigned tasks, observing blockers/returns, choosing next interventions and distributing execution across models/roles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked or failing tasks, exhausted backends, stalled review chains, quota pressure and competing commitments over a finite worker pool. - Whole-system current view: project orchestrator obtains series/task state, completion recaps, blocked tasks and role/series plans; source explicitly seeds the orchestrator to follow rather than itself execute worker code. - Current-control decision scope: the model-backed project/main orchestrator can replan the active work, send diagnostic/recovery missions, abandon or escalate blocked series; in an operator-plan mode the user selects model/provider resources for the whole task series. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which current task/role needs attention and which commitment and resource allocation the project should pursue next. - Decision owner: in the base mode, the model-backed orchestrator whose mission receives synthesized returns; in the parent mode, the human selects the series plan&#39;s per-role/provider allocations and the running orchestrator applies those choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task database, admission caps, role routing, resource watchdog and fixed day/night quotas, backoff, wake-trigger scheduling, model-selection validation. - Closure path: worker completion/failure triggers recap, the orchestrator receives it and chooses a next task or escalation; the UI&#39;s human-selected series plan returns to the agent as commands, and the agent applies role/provider choices before the tasks are dispatched. - Boundary reachability: orchestrator seeding and wake/dispatcher are standard first-party runtime; the series-plan UI question and per-role configuration are shipped, not a possible future plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in the base mode, fixed resource caps do not select the substantive intervention; the agent does. In the distinct parent mode, the human&#39;s whole-series allocation judgment is the deciding authority, not merely a permission to execute a shell command. - Evidence: orchestrator role and its decisions; wake recaps and escalation; series-plan user choices and applied commands; series-wide role/provider enforcement; resource-only enforcement. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the parent mode is limited to the explicitly supported user-controlled series-wide provider/task allocation; a generic allow/deny card, service restart or fixed hardware cap is not counted as S3 governance. No live mission traces were executed.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge the coder&#39;s ordinary completed-task claim by independent examination of the actual changed Git state before integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an agent can report completion while its patch has defects, violates requirements or fails correctness/security review. - Claim being audited: the coder&#39;s code task and underlying branch are fit to merge. - Ordinary reporting path: coder&#39;s task closure and mission recap. - Complementary access path: separately seeded reviewer role directly reads branch diff/commit evidence, returns verdict: pass or verdict: changesrequested with actionable findings. - Independence boundary: reviewer has a different role prompt and task identity, is explicitly barred from editing, and uses underlying Git diff rather than only trusting coder&#39;s done comment. Separate model identity is configurable and need not imply independent model vendors. - Who acts on findings: verification-loop parses the reviewer judgment; a failed review creates a bounded new code iteration with feedback, and pass creates a merge task; invalid verdict blocks/escalates rather than silently integrating. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent substantive judgment of whether the actual patch passes or needs changes. - Decision owner: a separate model-backed reviewer actor in the ordinary configured code/review/merge pipeline; the parser and verification loop only enforce its verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: diff-stat preparation, review role tool restrictions, verdict parser, iteration cap, merge dependency and logs. - Closure path: reviewer outcome changesrequested feeds into the next coder iteration; a pass unlocks the merger; unparseable review blocks and notifies the supervising orchestrator. - Boundary reachability: handleCodeDone explicitly generates a review task and resolves/seeds a reviewer in the shipped full pipeline. It can be intentionally disabled in code-only mode, which is not credited as an S3 mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a bounded, read-only independent reviewer makes the quality judgment on complementary raw source evidence; deterministic parser/guard alone would not decide defect severity. The system&#39;s own patch audit is distinct from external security audit provided as a product task. - Evidence: reviewer identity, read-only rules and verdict contract; code-to-review-to-repair/merge closure; verdict parser; dependency closure gate. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: no empirical independence or recall measurement; a reviewer can share a provider family with the coder. Code-only and no-diff paths do not provide this audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: produce prospective project-improvement and external-opportunity options relative to the owner&#39;s stated longer-term objectives. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing outside market/tool opportunities, future project requirements and mismatch between existing portfolio and objectives. - External distinction: the bundled opportunity phase explicitly uses WebSearch/WebFetch for external trends, demand and tools; improvement phase combines current project scores, prior failures and accepted objectives. - Future / prospective distinction: nightly dreaming generates options, cost/conviction rationales and first steps for potential later work, rather than just responding to an immediate operational task. - Adaptation option generated: an OSS-shipped dreamproposals queue contains prospective suggestions, source links and human review actions. - Path back into current capability / S3: an operator can accept/reject/snooze the specific proposed option in the bundled Dreams UI; acceptance is recorded, but the inspected code does not itself create an executing Kanban task, reconfigure the agent&#39;s current capability or automatically integrate source. That subsequent implementation/commitment loop requires a human/developer composition. - Decisive decision or feedback right: assess future environment signals and select action-worthy options for the project portfolio. - Decision owner: shipped dedicated agent prompt generates the options, but final prioritization/translation of accepted proposals into present controlled commitments is left to an operator. This establishes a function-specific S4 constructor path rather than an autonomously closed S4 adaptation loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: nightly timer, raw project score, deduplication, proposal database, notification, web tools, human status route. - Closure path: prospective proposals reach a structured review queue; the absence of automatic activation into present work limits the state to C, not A or P. - Boundary reachability: startDreamer ships in the daemon and the Dreams route/UI includes accepting/rejecting proposals. A configured supported provider with WebSearch/WebFetch is needed for live external research. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses future options but source does not show it owning a complete adaptation decision that changes current capability; a generic browser or memory file alone would not be enough, whereas this future-oriented, external-evidence-to-proposal path is explicit. - Evidence: nightly prospective web research, proposal insert; human accept/reject route; Dreams UI decisions; project priority signals. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: C does not grant automatic or closed autonomous adaptation; no evidence that accepting a dream automatically creates a resource-backed task. The separate internal memory-curation routine alone is not credited as external S4.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: possible owner-level identity/ultimate-policy guidance for a local cross-project agent team; a policy-issue decision and authoritative returned resolution are not decisively reconstructed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing project priorities and future directions can be reconciled against user-set objectives, but the boundary of an actual identity-policy issue versus normal task preference is unclear. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the team&#39;s ultimate direction in the face of strategic conflicts, not simply authorize an individual tool or choose a model. - Decision owner: the human user edits an objectives document and controls the acceptance of dream proposals; source establishes a possible parent direction, but not a bounded, issue-triggered S5 resolution transaction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: objectives.md, cross-project digest, project scorer, dream directives and approvals. - Closure path: the objectives document feeds the globalcontext agent&#39;s mission digest and scorer; however, the connection from an explicit identity-level issue or S3–S4 conflict to a resolved ultimate-policy choice and its enforced return is not established by the inspected material. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither routine tool approval nor editable objectives automatically establish S5; equally, the shipped objectives-to-cross-project-context path is concrete enough that this review does not assert a bounded absence without additional source/operating-mode evidence. - Evidence: objectives digest; conditional digest injection; objective-based scoring; dream directives and proposals; dream review route. - Basis: structural and unknown. - Confidence: low for a positive S5 claim; high for the described first-party signal paths. - Caveats: ? is specific to the potentially in-boundary objectives/policy loop and its unproven identity-level decision/closure, not a default negative state. OSS repo maintainer policy is an excluded higher recursion.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/91abaa6bf94a5fbb962e3c89fafe5acfcdd11707/assessments/ti/timon.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 91abaa6bf94a5fbb962e3c89fafe5acfcdd11707\">91abaa6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:05:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skrvidz/agentic-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agentic Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skrvidz/agentic-harness/tree/c67b6cec088d8f0054d9626adad6a22ea87fdbd6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c67b6cec088d8f0054d9626adad6a22ea87fdbd6\">c67b6ce &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-led project implementation via Claude or Codex engineer turns, including tool/repository feedback and task revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing local source and tests, uncertain implementation requirements, reviewer defects, failed commands and subsequent model observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose code edits, test/inspection commands, interpretation of feedback and implementation strategy inside the supplied objective/current task. - Decision owner: Spawned engineer model CLI as explicit task-operating actor, bounded by Harness and its model/tool permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ClaudeRunner/CodexEngineerRunner, persistent state, per-run worktrees, test evidence, max-cycles and reviewer feedback file. - Closure path: Human objective → engineer model turn and code edits → terminal/tool feedback → engine captures outcome → separate review → revised task fed into next engineer turn or final result. - Boundary reachability: agent-harness run and workspace Run create a real engineer subprocess, while --dry-run is explicitly separate and not credited for autonomous operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Engineer model chooses substantive implementation; removing it leaves only runner/orchestration and scripted test fixtures. - Evidence: CLI invocation, subprocess runners, engineer turn. - Basis: structural first-party executable path. - Confidence: High for decision-loop wiring, medium for actual provider behavior without an authenticated run. - Caveats: The harness does not implement provider transport; installed authenticated CLI models are required outside dry-run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No qualifying S2 loop that detects operational-unit interaction-induced disturbance and returns attenuation into independent peer behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel plan tickets may conflict during Git integration, or dependencies may block scheduling; these are prevented/flagged by fixed isolation/dependency checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Static scheduler selects ready DAG nodes; merge conflicts fail/pause rather than a distinct S2 coordinator negotiating or adapting peer commitments. - Decision owner: Model planner authored the initial task graph; deterministic PlanHarness enforces it with fixed width, worktrees and merge checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Validated dependson, ready[:width], bounded asyncio.gather, isolated TicketWorkspace and fail-on-conflict integration. - Closure path: Plan → independent ticket loops → deterministic merge or pause; no observed conflict signal → inter-S1 attenuation decision → peer behavior correction path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A dependency graph and isolation are generic task sequencing. Model planning is not proven to monitor competing peers and damp induced interference during current execution. - Evidence: DAG scheduler, worktrees/merge. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Model could have chosen safer dependencies ex ante, but a hypothetical conflict-attenuation behavior is not an evidenced first-party closed S2 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Current-goal control of implementation commitments and task direction based on whole-run state and independent evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Code that does not satisfy goal/acceptance tests, unresolved scope decisions, an incomplete increment, and uncertainty whether a wider ticket plan achieved the objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer decides APPROVED with a next task, REVISE with required changes, DONE or NEEDSHUMAN; the plan-level evaluator judges the integrated project against the whole original objective and may pause delivery. - Decision owner: Dedicated reviewer model for the single-run organization; in plan mode, dedicated planner/final-evaluator model for whole-ticket commitment judgment. Harness applies decisions to future task/current status. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed review schema, recorded run state/criteria, feedback file, nextactor transition, bounded cycle budget, SQLite event artifacts and final-plan evaluation. - Closure path: Current project evidence + engineer work → reviewer compares objective/criteria → chooses revision/next increment/completion/human gate → engine adjusts the next S1 task or stops. In DAG mode, integrated ticket results and source evidence → planner&#39;s final verdict → whole-goal completion or pause. - Whole-system current view: reviewerprompt receives the complete current objective/criteria and source-backed diff/status/test evidence; plannerfinalprompt additionally receives every ticket outcome, full plan and integrated-repo evidence. - Current-control decision scope: Approve a sound increment and redirect the next task, require corrective rework, declare current run complete or require human decision; final plan acceptance can be withheld. This is discretionary present-task commitment control, not a deterministic max-cycle or width guard. - Boundary reachability: Every default engineer turn is followed by reviewer control in Harness.runasync; plan mode invokes final model evaluation after all ticket integrations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects the quality/commitment verdict and next operational instruction from evidence; the Python engine merely branches on the verdict to update S1 commitments. Parent human involvement is a pause/answer gate, not claimed as a separate S3(P) mode. - Evidence: review verdict and actions, current-state prompts, final planner control. - Basis: explicit + structural first-party model choice and return path. - Confidence: Medium-high; model verdict rights are explicit though semantic quality is unobserved in live providers. - Caveats: No independent fleet budget arbitration claimed; scoped to goal commitment/current work in one configured harness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate evidence-facing review of operational completion claims and test outcomes before continued implementation or acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Engineer can confidently report a completed change that fails acceptance criteria, hides untracked files or contains regressions not captured in its summary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Read-only reviewer supplies structured findings and REVISE/DONE/APPROVED verdict from direct repository/test evidence; engine records it and routes corrections. - Decision owner: Distinct Codex reviewer CLI session (fresh read-only sandbox), separate from edit-capable engineer; the independent final planner evaluates integrated evidence in DAG mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: collectevidence runs real Git diff/status/untracked listing and optional project test command; review prompt calls engineer text untrusted; Codex --sandbox read-only and JSON schema; persisted audit raw artifacts. - Closure path: Engineer&#39;s report → independent evidence capture plus reviewer primary-source inspection → verdict and cited findings → reviewerfeedback.md/currenttask → next engineer turn, or blocked human decision/completion. - Claim being audited: Work delivered in the active turn meets the stated objective and acceptance criteria and can be accepted as done or as a sound increment. - Ordinary reporting path: The engineer summary and external CLI stream, persisted as pendingengineersummary and transcript artifacts. - Complementary access path: Git diff/stat/status and untracked file listing from the actual working tree, configured test-command output and reviewer direct read-only access to the code, plus independent integrated working-tree evidence for final plan review. - Independence boundary: Separate subprocess agent and session; fresh Codex reviewers start under read-only sandbox, cannot directly modify engineer&#39;s work. Captured evidence is provided by harness code, not inferred from engineer text; resumed sessions retain prior policy. - Who acts on findings: Harness.applyreview converts REVISE into the next engineer assignment; NEEDSHUMAN forces a human question; DONE gates completion. Planner can withhold whole-plan completion. - Boundary reachability: Review is mandatory after each successful engineer turn in default Harness, with nextactor=reviewer durable even when the cycle limit is exhausted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent evidence-backed reviewer judgments are not the same as generic logs or unit tests; they determine whether the operation is revised, continued or completed. - Evidence: evidence collection, read-only reviewer runner, review feedback return. - Basis: explicit + structural independent reviewer path. - Confidence: High for separation, evidence path and corrective wiring; no provider-backed audit of actual generated changes performed here. - Caveats: Missing configured test command is marked unverified; evidence bounded/truncated to 40k chars, and a read-only CLI sandbox still relies on vendor client enforcement outside the first-party product.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective organizational adaptation that changes future harness capabilities in response to an external environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future implementation milestones are decomposed from the present task; no separate environmental foresight is continuously collected to change agent organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model planner defines the current goal&#39;s ticket list, but cannot alter the harness&#39;s future capability, governance or strategy; user changes model/settings manually. - Decision owner: Planner for near-term task plan and human for future system configuration; no S4 actor established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DAG plan/review, stored transcripts and model selection configuration. - Closure path: Goal → model-authored near-term tickets → implementation; no future environment scan → adaptation option → selection → return to future operating capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Time-ordered ticket planning is present operational decomposition, not strategic outside-and-then environmental intelligence. - Evidence: planner prompt, DAG execution. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An outside organization could adopt findings to redesign its tooling, but that return is not wired in the assessed package.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No recurring ultimate organizational identity and policy arbitration loop within the product. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe edits, unsupported decisions and risky plan changes are bounded by configured CLI sandbox/limits and human pause. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human sets objective and decides blocked product/scope questions; no first-party actor adjudicates enduring identity or constitutional policy. - Decision owner: Human user for objective and particular decision; editor/CLI sandbox for fixed access bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Human decision callback/CLI, max cycles, explicit noninteractive pause, read-only reviewer setting and worktree constraints. - Closure path: Risk/product question → human decision → next engineer task, or configured hard limit → pause; neither constitutes policy identity dispute → ultimate arbitration → binding organizational-policy change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Operational question/approval and fixed sandbox policy are not S5 policy-authority decision rights. - Evidence: human decision handling, README safety boundary. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: User&#39;s employer or external host may retain ultimate policy authority outside this local harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/717a8b4606aa4ea034e6108cf2200e696f90de56/assessments/ag/agentic-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 717a8b4606aa4ea034e6108cf2200e696f90de56\">717a8b4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
